We conclude our review of systemic risk measures by providing examples of single-element aggregation functions that we will revisit in Section~\ref{sec:nash} below. We note that we also consider a different aggregation function based on financial clearing conditions in Section~\ref{sec:EN}.

\begin{example}\label{ex:se-aggregation}
Here are a few examples of single-element aggregation functions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item\label{util} \textbf{Sum of univariate utilities:} For each $i\in[N]$, let $u_i\colon\R\to\R\cup\{-\infty\}$ be a \emph{univariate utility function}, i.e., an increasing upper semicontinuous function such that $\dom u_i\neq \emptyset$. Let $\bar{\Lambda}^U(x)\coloneqq\sum_{i \in [N]} u_i(x_i)$ for each $x\in\R^N$. As presented in, e.g., \cite{biagini2019unified,biagini2020fairness}, the function $\bar\Lambda^U$ is a single-element aggregation function with $\dom\bar\Lambda^U=\bigtimes_{i \in [N]} \dom u_i\neq\emptyset$.
% Preview of future: $\bar\Lambda^u_i(x) = u_i(x_i)$
%$\dom\bar\Lambda_i^U=\dom\bar\Lambda^U$ is a closed set
\item\label{fof} \textbf{Fund of funds:} As detailed in \citet[Example 3]{chen2013axiomatic}, when an individual invests with a hedge fund, they are charged a fee on their gains. That is, a gain of $x_i\in\R$ in firm $i\in[N]$ has a payout of $x_i - \gamma_i x_i^+$, where $\gamma_i \in [0,1)$ is the fee rate for firm $i$. However, it is possible for the investor to not just invest in individual firms, but in a \emph{fund of funds}, where an additional $\gamma \in [0,1)$ fraction of the profits of that aggregate fund is taken by the fund administrator. From this construction, we define
\[
\bar\Lambda^{FoF}(x) := \sum_{i \in [N]} (x_i - \gamma_i x_i^+) - \gamma \of{\sum_{i \in [N]} (x_i - \gamma_i x_i^+)}^+
\]
for each $x\in\R^N$. Clearly, $\bar\Lambda^{FoF}$ is a continuous single-element aggregation function with $\dom\bar\Lambda^{FoF}=\R^N$.
% Preview of future: $\bar\Lambda^{FoF}_i(x) := x_i - \gamma_i x_i^+ - \gamma(x_i - \gamma x_i^+)^+$
\item\label{mf} \textbf{Mean-field utility:} Inspired by mean-field games (see, e.g., \citet[Section~3.1]{carmonaMF} and \citet[Section~2]{bensoussanLQ}), we introduce the idea that each bank's utility depends on itself and the average health of the financial system. Mathematically, each bank $i\in [N]$ has utility $u_i(x_i , \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j \in [N]} x_j)$ so that the utility of bank $i$ depends on itself ($x_i$) and an aggregate of the financial system ($\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j \in [N]} x_j$), where $u_i\colon\R\times\R\to\R\cup\{-\infty\}$ is an aggregation function with $\dom u_i = A_i\times \bar{A}$ for some $A_i\in \mathcal{R}(\R;\R_+)\setminus\{\emptyset\}$ (depending on $i$) and $\bar{A}\in\mathcal{R}(\R;\R_+)\setminus\{\emptyset\}$ (independent of $i$). We also assume that $\bigtimes_{i\in[N]}A_i \subseteq \{x\in\R^N\;|\; \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j\in[N]}x_j\in \bar{A}\}$. %for fixed $(x_{j})_{j\in [N]\setminus\{i\}}$, the function $x_i \mapsto u_i(x_i , \frac{1}{N}[x_i + \sum_{j \neq i} x_j])$ is increasing. 
As in the case of sum of univariate utilities, we define 
\[
\bar\Lambda^{MF}(x) := \sum_{i \in [N]} u_i\of{x_i , \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j \in [N]} x_j}
\]
for each $x\in\R^N$, which is a single-element aggregation function with $\dom \bar\Lambda^{MF}=\bigtimes_{i\in[N]}A_i$.
