\subsection{Cluster Variables}
Here, we will show that each $X_\fpq$ is in fact the cluster variable $x_\fpq$ in the (possibly generalized) cluster algebra $\mathcal{A}^\prin(k_1,k_2,k_3)$ labeled by $\fpq$. 
This is known when $k_1 = k_2 = k_3 = 0$, i.e. when we have an ordinary cluster algebra, by \cite{pilaud2023posets}.
By setting $y_1=y_2=y_3 = 1$, we also will get an interpretation of cluster variables in $\mathcal{A}(k_1,k_2,k_3)$. Note that we can extend our fractional labeling from $\mathcal{A}(k_1,k_2,k_3)$ to $\mathcal{A}^\prin(k_1,k_2,k_3)$ using the canonical isomorphism between their cluster patterns. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:CorrectnessOfPosetFormula}
Let $\fpq \in \mathbb{Q} \cap [0,\infty)$. The cluster variable $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p}{q}}$ is given by \[
x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p}{q}} = \mathbf{x}^{\gb_{\frac{p}{q}}} \mathcal{W}^{\prin}(\calP_{\frac{p}{q}}).
\]
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof}
Let $F_{1,\fpq}$ denote the $F$-polynomial associated to $x^{\prin}_{1,\fpq}$. Let $\hat{y_i}:=  y_ix_1^{b_{1i}}x_2^{b_{2i}}x_3^{b_{3i}}$ and let $F_\fpq = F_{1,\fpq}(\hat{y_1},\hat{y_2},\hat{y_3})$.  From Theorem \ref{thm:SeparationFormulaNakanishi}, Theorem \ref{thm:g-vector-description},   Lemma \ref{lem:CompareGVector}, and \cite[Proposition 3.19]{nakanishi2015structure}, which states that $F$-polynomials are polynomials with constant term 1, it suffices to show that $F_{\frac{p}{q}} = \mathcal{W}^{\prin}(\calP_{\frac{p}{q}})$. 

Note that if $\fpq = \frac{0}{1}$ or $\frac10$, then the statement is immediately true since both polynomials are 1.  We can also check the claim for $\fpq \in \{ \frac{1}{1}, \frac{1}{2}, \frac21\}$ directly. 

Now, we proceed inductively.  Let $(\frac{p}{q},\frac{p+r}{q+s},\frs)$ be a Farey triple such that we already know $F_{\frac{p}{q}} = \mathcal{W}^\prin(\calP_{\frac{p}{q}})$,  $F_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}} = \mathcal{W}^\prin(\calP_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}})$, and $F_{\frac{r}{s}} = \mathcal{W}^\prin(\calP_{\frac{r}{s}})$. 
 If we mutate at $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p+r}{q+s}}$, we will reach a cluster closer to the initial cluster, and by induction we also know the desired statement for the resulting cluster variable. Therefore, we need only consider mutating at $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p}{q}}$ and at $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{r}{s}}$.   We focus first on mutating $x_{1,\frac{p}{q}}$.  From the Farey operation,  we know the mutation of $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p}{q}}$ is $x^{\prin}_{1,\frac{p+2r}{q+2s}}$.   

Let $d_\fpq$ be the degree of the mutation polynomial associated to $x_{1,\fpq}$. From the alternate definition of $F$-polynomials in Definition \ref{def:FPoly} and the sign pattern of the $B$-matrix described in Lemma \ref{lem:BMatrixUsingLabeling}, if $d_{\frac{p}{q}} = 1$, we have \begin{align}
F_{\frac{p+2r}{q+2s}}F_{\frac{p}{q}} = F_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}}^2  +  \hat{\yy}^{\cc_{\frac{p}{q}}}  F_{\frac{r}{s}}^2\label{eq:MutateOrdinary}
\end{align}
and if $d_{\frac{p}{q}} = 2$,   we have that \begin{align}
F_{\frac{p+2r}{q+2s}}F_{\frac{p}{q}} = F_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}}^2 + k_\frac{p}{q} \hat{\yy}^{\cc_{\frac{p}{q}}} F_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}} F_{\frac{r}{s}} +  \hat{\yy}^{2\cc_{\frac{p}{q}}}  F_{\frac{r}{s}}^2\label{eq:MutateGen}
\end{align}

where $\cc_{\frac{p}{q}}$ denotes the $c$-vector associated to $x^\prin_{1,\frac{p}{q}}$ in the seed in $k_1 \mathbb{T}(k_1,k_2,k_3)$ associated to $(\frac{p}{q},\frac{p+r}{q+s},\frs)$.  From Proposition \ref{prop:MarkovCVectors} and Lemma \ref{lem:CVectorsGenMarkov}  we have \[
\cc_{\frac{p}{q}} = \bigg(\frac{d_1 (p+q+1)}{d_{\frac{p}{q}}} ,\frac{d_2 (q+1)}{d_{\frac{p}{q}}},  \frac{d_3 (p+1)}{d_{\frac{p}{q}}}\bigg)^\top.
\]

Once we show that the polynomials $\mathcal{W}^\prin(\calP)$ have the same relationship, we will be done by induction.  

Assume first that $\fpq \neq \frac01$ and $\frs \neq \frac10$. From Lemma \ref{lem:ShapeOfPosetInFareyTriple}, we know $\calP_{\frac{p+2r}{q+2s}}$ is of the form $\overline{\mathcal P}_{\frac{r}{s}} \searrow \overline{H} \searrow \overline{\mathcal P}_{\frac{p+r}{q+s}}$. Using this Lemma again on $\overline{\mathcal P}_{\frac{p+q}{r+s}}$ shows us that $\calP_{\frac{p+2r}{q+2s}}$ is of the following form where the chains $2 \cdots 3$ represent, from left to right,  $\overline{H}$ and $H$.

