In this example, Ann may suffer from public information.
First, Bob's centrality is $c_B(\ga, G)=1/(1-\ga \beta)$, while Ann's centrality is $c_A(\ga, G)=(1+\ga (\alpha-\beta))/(1-\ga \beta)$. Plugging these into  (\ref{inequalityindividual}) and multiplying it by $(1-\ga\beta)^2$ yields
\begin{equation}
f(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\equiv (1+\ga (\alpha-\beta))^2-\alpha(2\ga\beta-1)<0.\label{equationABC}
\end{equation}
