\section*{Limitations and Formal Scope}

While the results presented here are internally consistent and fully constructive, the termination proof via the ranking function $\rho(n)$ is contingent upon the correctness of the underlying symbolic model. Specifically, the following limitations apply with regard to formal embedding in foundational axiomatic systems:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The argument is developed within a symbolic framework, in which a digitwise automaton emulates the classical Collatz function $T(n)$ through local, deterministic state transitions.

  \item All symbolic operations — including digit sum, parity evaluation, carry logic, and the transition function $\delta$ — are primitive recursive and thus representable in first-order Peano Arithmetic (PA). However, the derivation of their arithmetic equivalence to $T(n)$ from PA axioms has not been formalized in this work.

  \item Consequently, the well-founded descent of $\rho(n)$ guarantees termination only under the assumption that each symbolic trajectory faithfully reproduces the arithmetic sequence $T^{(k)}(n)$. This assumption is supported by constructive correctness (cf. Theorem~\ref{thm:encoding}) but remains unverified in a proof-theoretic sense.
\end{itemize}

This symbolic approach departs fundamentally from analytic and number-theoretic strategies. It offers a finite, deterministic, and verifiable reformulation of the Collatz dynamics, and suggests new directions for formalization within logical frameworks, including proof assistants and bounded arithmetic.



\section{Conclusion}

We have introduced a finite state symbolic automaton that emulates the Collatz function through local digitwise transitions. Each digit is encoded as a symbolic triple, and the system operates deterministically on base ten sequences. This automaton reproduces the arithmetic behavior of \(T(n)\) exactly and enables a structural decomposition of Collatz dynamics at the digit level. The symbolic framework is fully constructive and finite, with local transition rules that lend themselves naturally to implementation in symbolic processors or low-level arithmetic logic. Because all operations are primitive recursive, the system is also amenable to formal verification in bounded arithmetic or mechanized proof assistants such as Lean. This enables both theoretical analysis and practical applications in symbolic computing, formal methods, and educational tools for discrete dynamical systems. To establish convergence formally, we defined a primitive recursive ranking function \(\rho(n)\), which is integer-valued, bounded below, and guaranteed to decrease after finitely many steps along every Collatz trajectory. While \(\rho(n)\) may increase temporarily—particularly after odd steps—it strictly decreases once the digit length drops. This descent ensures termination and enables a proof by well-founded induction. The function \(\rho\) is fully representable in Peano Arithmetic and provides a constructive foundation for global convergence. Future work may include formalization in proof assistants such as Lean, generalizations to other numeric bases, or symbolic models for related recursive maps. These results suggest that arithmetic behavior, even in classically resistant problems, can be expressed and verified through finite symbolic mechanisms.


