\subsection{ Primitive Recursive Definition of the Transition Function}

The transition function $\delta_i(r_i, p_i)$ used in the symbolic automaton is given by:
\[
\delta_i(r_i, p_i) := \left\lfloor \frac{r_i}{2} \right\rfloor + 5p_i,
\]
where $r_i \in \{0,2,4,6,8\}$ and $p_i \in \{0,1\}$.

This function is defined only for even input digits and produces valid decimal outputs. It compactly encodes the ten-case lookup table used during symbolic halving with parity offset.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cc}
$r_i$ & $p_i = 0$ & $p_i = 1$ \\
\hline
0 & 0 & 5 \\
2 & 1 & 6 \\
4 & 2 & 7 \\
6 & 3 & 8 \\
8 & 4 & 9 \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All operations involved are primitive recursive:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Parity: $ r_{i+1} \bmod 2$
    \item Integer division: $\lfloor r_i / 2 \rfloor$
    \item Addition and multiplication
    \item Carry propagation
    \item Digit summation and length computation
\end{itemize}

Hence, the entire symbolic emulation of $T(n)$ via $\delta$ is primitive recursive and definable in Peano Arithmetic (PA).

\subsection{Symbolic Evaluation for \boldmath$n = 32$ (Even Case)}
\label{appendix:even-example}

We illustrate the symbolic digitwise emulation for the even input \( n = 32 \), where \( T(32) = 16 \).

The decimal expansion is:
\[
n = 2 \cdot 10^0 + 3 \cdot 10^1 \quad \Rightarrow \quad r_0 = 2,\quad r_1 = 3
\]

We now compute the updated digits from right to left:

\begin{align*}
p_0 &= r_1 \bmod 2 = 3 \bmod 2 = 1 \\
c_0 &= 0 \quad \text{(by definition)} \\
r_0' &= \delta_{\text{even}}(2, p_0, c_0) = \left\lfloor \frac{2 - 0}{2} \right\rfloor + 5 \cdot 1 = 1 + 5 = 6 \\[1ex]
p_1 &= 0 \quad \text{(no digit after \( r_1 \))} \\
c_1 &= r_1 \bmod 2 = 3 \bmod 2 = 1 \\
r_1' &= \delta_{\text{even}}(3, p_1, c_1) = \left\lfloor \frac{3 - 1}{2} \right\rfloor + 5 \cdot 0 = 1 + 0 = 1
\end{align*}

We obtain the updated digit sequence \( (r_0', r_1') = (6, 1) \), which corresponds to:
\[
T(32) = 6 \cdot 10^0 + 1 \cdot 10^1 = 16
\]

\begin{remark}
This confirms that the symbolic transition faithfully emulates the even-case arithmetic of the Collatz map at the digit level, including parity-aware carry handling.
\end{remark}



