\subsection{Derivation and Motivation of the Ranking Function $\rho(n)$}

To ensure termination of the symbolic Collatz sequence, we define a ranking function:
\[
\rho(n) := M \cdot \ell(n) + \mathrm{digit\_sum}(n),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\ell(n) = \lfloor \log_{10}(n) \rfloor + 1$ is the number of decimal digits,
    \item $\mathrm{digit\_sum}(n) = \sum r_i$, the sum of the digits in base 10,
    \item $M \in \mathbb{N}$ is a fixed constant with $M > 9$.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Why $M > 9$?} 
Each decimal digit contributes at most 9 to the digit sum. A decrease in digit count $\ell(n)$ may increase the digit sum. Choosing $M > 9$ ensures that a drop in $\ell(n)$ always dominates.

\paragraph{Example: $n = 100 \to 50$ (valid Collatz step)}
\[
\rho(100) = 3M + 1, \quad \rho(50) = 2M + 5
\]
To ensure $\rho(50) < \rho(100)$, we need $M > 4$. More generally, $M > 9$ guarantees this in all cases.

\section*{Dynamic Weighting in $\rho(n)$}

While the main definition of the ranking function
\[
\rho(n) = M \cdot \ell(n) + \text{digit\_sum}(n)
\]
relies on a fixed constant \( M > 9 \), one could consider a dynamic variant where \( M \) adapts to the number length:

\[
M(n) := 9 \cdot \ell(n)
\]

This guarantees that any reduction in digit length yields a strictly greater decrease in the weighted length term than the maximum possible increase in digit sum. Formally, for any \( n \in \mathbb{N}_{>0} \), this ensures:
\[
\rho(n) > \rho(T(n))
\]
whenever \( \ell(T(n)) < \ell(n) \).

However, making \( M \) dependent on \( n \) violates the requirement of a fixed, primitive recursive function. For formalization in Peano Arithmetic (PA), we therefore use a global constant \( M > 9 \), which is sufficient to ensure descent in all relevant cases.



