    \field{abstract}{%
    The morphogenesis of developing embryos and organs relies on the ability of
  cells to remodel their contacts with neighbouring cells. Using quantitative
  modelling and laser nano-dissection, we probed the mechanics of a
  morphogenetic process, the elongation of Drosophila melanogaster embryos,
  which results from polarized cell neighbour exchanges. We show that
  anisotropy of cortical tension at apical cell junctions is sufficient to
  drive tissue elongation. We estimated its value through comparisons between
  in silico and in vivo data using various tissue descriptors. Nano-dissection
  of the actomyosin network indicates that tension is anisotropically
  distributed and depends on myosin II accumulation. Junction relaxation after
  nano-dissection also suggests that cortical elastic forces are dominant in
  this process. Interestingly, fluctuations in vertex position (points where
  three or more cells meet) facilitate neighbour exchanges. We delineate the
  contribution of subcellular tensile activity polarizing junction remodelling,
  and the permissive role of vertex fluctuations during tissue elongation.%
    }
