Generally, the polarizability can be decomposed into three contributions: the scalar, the vector, and the tensor polarizability. However, due to the spherical symmetry of the bosonic strontium clock states, the vector and tensorial terms vanish. Thus, the polarizability take the following polarization-independent form, 
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_i(\omega_L) = \frac{4\pi}{3h}\sum_n\frac{\omega_{ni}}{\omega_{ni}^2-\omega_L^2} | \bra {i}|D|\ket{n}|^2,   
 \label{eq:polarizability}
\end{equation}
where $\omega_{ni}$ is the angular frequency of the bare transition $i\rightarrow n$, $D$ is the electric dipole operator, and $h$ the Planck constant. The equation clearly indicates that the transitions with nearby resonant frequencies give the dominant contribution. These transitions are shown in \fig{Intro}(b) and their respective properties are listed in Table~\ref{tab:dominant_transitions}. With \eq{polarizability} and  Table~\ref{tab:dominant_transitions}, we compute the polarizabilities of the clock states around 477 nm, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:freq_pol}.

