\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In inverse optimization problems, the goal is, broadly speaking, to modify certain parameters of an optimization problem such that the resulting optimal aligns with a given fixed feasible set. Due to their applicability in both theory and practice, inverse problems have garnered significant interest in past decades; we refer the interested reader to~\cite{richter2016inverse, heuberger2004inverse,demange2014introduction, chan2023inverse} for surveys.

Let $S$ be a ground set and $\cF\subseteq 2^S$ be the set of feasible solutions. Given weights $w\in \bR^S$, we denote by $(S,\cF,w)$ the optimization problem of finding a maximum-weight member of $\cF$ with respect to $w$. By an \emph{objective function} $\|\cdot\|$, we mean a function from $\bR^S$ to $\bR$ that measures the magnitude of a vector, such as a norm. Then, a classical inverse optimization problem can formally be described as follows.

\problemdef{Inverse Optimization (\textsc{IO})}
    {A finite ground set $S$, a collection $\cF \subseteq 2^S$ of feasible solutions for an underlying optimization problem, an input solution $F^* \in \cF$, a weight function $w \in \bR^S$, an objective function $\| \cdot \|$ defined on $\bR^S$, and an oracle $\mathcal{O}$ for the maximization problem $(S, \cF, w')$ for any weight function $w'\in\bR^S$.}
    {Find a new weight function $w^* \in \bR^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item \label{it:IOP_a} $F^*$ is a maximum-weight member of $\cF$ with respect to $w^*$, and
        \item \label{it:IOP_c} $\|w-w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}
A weight function that has property~\ref{it:IOP_a} is called \emph{feasible}, and it is called \emph{optimal} if additionally \ref{it:IOP_c} holds. For an optimal weight function $w^*$, its \emph{optimal value} is $\delta^* \coloneqq \|w-w^*\|$. 

Motivated by applications in dynamic pricing and partial inverse problems, we introduce several extensions of \textsc{IO} where, instead of a fixed solution, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ is specified. The goal is to modify the weight function so that at least one, all, or exactly the feasible solutions contained in $S_0$ become maximum-weight. We also consider the corresponding negated variants, resulting in six distinct problems that capture different requirements on the structure of optimal solutions.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Our results and techniques
\label{sec:our_results}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this paper, we focus on the \textsc{Inverse Matroid} (\textsc{IM}) problem under $\ell_\infty$-norm -- a special case of \textsc{IO} where the feasible set $\cF$ consists of the bases of a matroid and the goal is to make a given basis maximum-weight while minimizing the $\ell_\infty$-norm of the deviation from the original weights. 

In Section~\ref{sec:offline}, we revisit a result by Bérczi, Mendoza-Cadena, and Varga~\cite{berczi2023infinity} on general inverse optimization problems. We present a simple algorithm for \textsc{IM}, achieving better running time than earlier methods (Theorem~\ref{thm:im}). At the same time, we give a refinement of the min-max theorem of~\cite{berczi2023infinity} in the matroid setting (Corollary~\ref{cor:IM-offline-infty_symmDiff_equals_2}).

Then we turn our attention to inverse matroid problems under subset constraints, where instead of a fixed basis, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ of the ground set is given. In Section~\ref{sec:IM-Exists}, we study the \textsc{Inverse Matroid Exists (IM-Exists)} problem, where the goal is to modify the weight function so that $S_0$ contains a maximum-weight basis. Revealing structural properties of optimal solutions (Lemmas~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_special_weight} and~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_matroid_infty_2delta}), we present two algorithms: one based on binary search (Theorem~\ref{thm:IM-Exists_binarySearch}) and another via reduction to \textsc{IM} (Theorem~\ref{thm:IM-Exists-combAlgo}).

Section~\ref{sec:IM-All} studies the \textsc{Inverse Matroid All (IM-All)} and \textsc{Only (IM-Only)} problems. In \textsc{IM-All}, the goal is to modify the weights so that all bases in $S_0$ become maximum-weight; \textsc{IM-Only} additionally requires that no other basis is optimal. Using a structural result on optimal solutions (Lemma~\ref{lem:hm}), we give a two-step algorithm for \textsc{IM-All} involving a homogenization phase to equalize the weights of bases in $S_0$, followed by a maximization step to ensure optimality (Theorem~\ref{thm:IM-All_algorithm}). A similar strategy yields a solution for \textsc{IM-Only} (Theorem~\ref{thm:IM-Only_algorithm}); here, we consider integral weight functions only to ensure that an optimal solution exists. These results are somewhat surprising, as controlling the weight of a fixed set of bases appears difficult at first glance -- yet it becomes tractable when the set includes all bases contained in a given subset.

The remainder of the paper is devoted to the negated versions of these problems, where we again add integrality constraints to ensure the existence of optimal solutions. Section~\ref{sec:not-IM-Exists} focuses on \textsc{Inverse Matroid Not Exists}, where the goal is to modify the weights so that no maximum-weight basis is contained in $S_0$. We approach the problem in multiple steps: first, we handle simple base cases (Theorem~\ref{lem:Not-IM-Exists_easy_case}), then introduce a relaxed intermediate problem that might be of independent interest (Theorem~\ref{thm:algo-relaxedProb}), which ultimately leads to a full solution (Theorem~\ref{thm:NOT-IM-Exists-algorithm}).

Section~\ref{sec:not-IM-All} introduces the \textsc{Inverse Matroid Not All (IM-Not-All)} and \textsc{Not Only (IM-Not-Only)} problems. In \textsc{IM-Not-All}, the goal is to modify the weights so that not all bases in $S_0$ are maximum-weight. The \textsc{IM-Not-Only} problem relaxes this requirement by allowing either some basis in $S_0$ to be non-maximum-weight or the existence of a maximum-weight basis that is not contained in $S_0$. To solve these problems, we first analyze the structure of optimal solutions (Lemma~\ref{lem:not}), and then deduce efficient algorithms for both problems (Theorem~\ref{thm:NOT-IM-All-algorithm} and Corollary~\ref{cor:NOT-IM-All-solution}).

Finally, we conclude the paper with a list of open problems in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}. Our algorithms rely on several intermediate subproblems, such as finding a weight function that assigns the same weight to all bases of a matroid, or finding one that ensures a maximum-weight basis is not contained in a given set. While these problems are not difficult, they serve as key building blocks for efficient algorithms and are thus of particular interest. Table~\ref{tab:results} shows a summary of our main results. 

It is worth emphasizing that the running time of our algorithms for the newly introduced problems is comparable to that of the \textsc{IM} problem. This is somewhat surprising in light of the fact that these problems are significantly more general.

