%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Further implications}
\label{sec:implications}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The six problems presented in the previous section explore different aspects of the inverse matroid problem under subset constraints. It is worth noting, however, that the framework we introduce applies to a much wider range of problems. Let $M$ be a matroid over ground set $S$, and let $S_0 \subseteq S$ be a subset. Depending on whether we apply the problems to $M$ or its dual $M^*$, and whether we consider $S_0$ or its complement $S \setminus S_0$, different problem variants arise.

For example, applying \textsc{IM-Exists} to the dual matroid $M^*$ and the set $S \setminus S_0$ allows us to find a weight function for which $M^*$ has a maximum-weight basis contained in $S \setminus S_0$; this is equivalent to $M$ having a minimum-weight basis containing $S_0$. Similarly, applying \textsc{IM-All} to $M^*$ and the set $S_0$ lets us find a weight function so that all bases of $M^*$ contained in $S_0$ are maximum-weight; equivalently, all bases of $M$ containing $S \setminus S_0$ have minimum weight.

The six base problems (\textsc{IM-Exists, IM-All, IM-Only, IM-Not-Exists, IM-Not-All, IM-Not-Only}), combined with the choice of $M$ or $M^*$ and exchanging the roles of $S_0$ and its complement $S \setminus S_0$, yield 24 different problems -- all of which can be solved by one of our algorithms. In particular, our framework can be used for solving partial inverse problems; see Section~\ref{sec:motiv} for further details.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Related work and motivation}
\label{sec:motiv}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Inverse matroid problems under subset constraints are closely related to several areas in combinatorial optimization. In the following, we provide a brief overview of previous work and related topics.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\paragraph{Inverse matroid problem.}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The \textsc{Inverse Matroid} problem was first introduced by Cai~\cite{mao1999inverse}, who studied matroid intersection under the $\ell_\infty$-norm. Dell'Amico, Maffioli, and Malucelli~\cite{dell2003base} later considered the problem under the $\ell_1$-norm and proposed an auxiliary matroid construction. Variants of the problem have since been explored under other distance measures, including weighted Hamming~\cite{aman2016inverse} and Chebyshev distances~\cite{tayybi2021inverse}. More generally, inverse optimization under $\ell_\infty$ and $\ell_1$ norms has been studied for 0-1 linear programs~\cite{zhang1999further, Ahuja2001Inverse, zhang2002general}, which include \textsc{Inverse Matroid} as a special case.

Beyond classical matroid structures, recent work has considered inverse optimization over general set systems. Bérczi, Mendoza-Cadena, and Varga~\cite{berczi2023infinity, berczi2024spanLATIN} studied inverse problems under the $\ell_\infty$-norm and the span objectives, where the feasible solutions are not necessarily defined by matroids. This framework includes matroids as a special case and applies to a wider range of combinatorial problems.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\paragraph{Inverse problems with forced and forbidden elements.}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Partial inverse optimization problems aim to adjust weights so that a desired subset of elements is either included in or excluded from an optimal solution. In the matroid setting, Li, Zhang, and Lai~\cite{li2016algorithm} and Zhang, Li, Lai, and Du~\cite{zhang2016partialmatroid} considered the problem of modifying weights (only decreasing or only increasing, respectively) so that a maximum-weight basis contains a given independent set. More generally, similar problems have been studied for spanning trees, cuts, and matchings~\cite{dong2022partial, li2019capacited, li2020approximation, li2022partial, li2021capacited}, with different complexities depending on the structure of the constraints and the norm used as an objective~\cite{gassner2010, lai2003complexity, gassner2009}. In the exclusion variant, where certain elements must be avoided in any optimal solution, many well-known problems become $\NP$-hard even under simple norms~\cite{lai2003complexity, gassner2009}. Ahmadian, Bhaskar,  Sanit{\`a}, and Swamy~\cite{ahmadian2018algorithms} considered a related problem where a subset $S_0\subseteq S$ is given, and the goal is to find a weight function with small $\ei$-norm (instead of minimizing the deviation from an initial weight function) for which the bases contained in $S_0$ are exactly the maximum-weight bases of the matroid. 

Inverse matroid problems with forced and forbidden elements fit naturally into our subset-constrained framework. Given a matroid $M$, an independent set $I$, and a set of forbidden elements $F$, the goal is to modify the weight function so that there exists a maximum-weight basis $B$ satisfying $I \subseteq B \subseteq S \setminus F$. This condition can be rephrased using the dual matroid $M^*$: we seek a basis $B^*$ such that $F \subseteq B^* \subseteq S \setminus I$. Hence, the problem reduces to an instance of \textsc{Inverse Matroid Exists} on the matroid obtained from $M^*$ by contracting $F$, with $S \setminus I$ as the target set $S_0$.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\paragraph{Dynamic pricing schemes.} 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

A combinatorial market consists of a set of indivisible items and a set of agents, where each agent has a valuation function assigning a value to each subset of items. Cohen-Addad, Eden, Feldman, and Fiat~\cite{cohen2016invisible} and independently Hsu, Morgenstern, Rogers, Roth, and Vohra~\cite{hsu2016prices} observed that Walrasian prices alone may not be enough to achieve optimal social welfare based solely on buyers' decisions. To address this,~\cite{cohen2016invisible} introduced the concept of dynamic pricing schemes. Unlike static models, the dynamic approach allows prices to be updated between buyer arrivals based on the remaining items and agents. A central open question posed in~\cite{cohen2016invisible} asked whether every market with gross substitutes valuations admits a dynamic pricing scheme that achieves optimal social welfare. Progress has been made in several special cases: the problem was resolved positively for matching markets~\cite{cohen2016invisible}, bi-demand markets~\cite{berczi2021market}, multi-demand markets with up to three~\cite{berger2020power} and four~\cite{pashkovich2022two} agents, and matching markets with fairness constraints~\cite{berczi2025envy}.

Since matroid rank functions form a subclass of gross substitutes valuations, a natural first step toward understanding the general case is to investigate whether a pricing scheme exists for markets with matroid rank valuations~\cite{berczi2021market}. In this setting, the problem can be reduced to the following inverse-type formulation: given $k$ matroids $M_1,\dots,M_k$ over the same ground set, find a weight function $w \in \mathbb{R}^S$ such that, for any maximum $w$-weight basis $B$ of $M_i$, the complement $S \setminus B$ is a basis of the sum of the remaining matroids. This problem resembles the setting where optimal bases are required to lie within a specified subset.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Organization} 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:preliminaries}, we introduce basic definitions, notation, and relevant results on matroids. Section~\ref{sec:offline} focuses on the \textsc{Inverse Matroid} problem under the $\ell_\infty$-norm, first giving an overview of earlier results from~\cite{berczi2023infinity} in Section~\ref{sec:offline_prep}, followed by an algorithm with improved running time nd a refinement of their min-max result. In Section~\ref{sec:IM-Exists}, we consider \textsc{Inverse Matroid Exists}. After some basic observations in Section~\ref{sec:prepexists}, we give a binary search algorithm in Section~\ref{sec:algpairs}, and then show a reduction to \textsc{Inverse Matroid} in Section~\ref{sec:algred}. The \textsc{Inverse Matroid All} and \textsc{Only} problems are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:IM-All}, with some structural observations described in Section~\ref{sec:prepall}, and algorithms given in Sections~\ref{sec:algall} and~\ref{sec:algonly}, respectively. The remainder of the paper studies the negated versions of these problems. Section~\ref{sec:not-IM-Exists} provides a solution for \textsc{Inverse Matroid Not Exists} in multiple steps. Easy base cases are tackled in Section~\ref{sec:easy}, while the general problem is solved via a relaxed version introduced in Section~\ref{sec:relaxed-not-IM-Exists}, leading to the algorithm of Section~\ref{sec:algorithm-not-IM-Exists}. Finally, solutions for the \textsc{Inverse Matroid Not All} and \textsc{Not Only} problems are given in Section~\ref{sec:not-IM-All}, where the structure of optimal solutions is described in Section~\ref{sec:prepnot}, and the algorithms are presented in Sections~\ref{sec:algnotall} and~\ref{sec:algnotonly}, respectively. We conclude the paper with a list of open problems in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:preliminaries}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Notation}
\label{sec:notation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We denote the sets of \emph{real} and \emph{integer} numbers by $\bR$ and $\bZ$, respectively, and add $+$ as a subscript whenever non-negativity is assumed. For a positive integer $k$, we use $[k]\coloneqq \{1,\dots,k\}$. Let $S$ be a ground set of size $n$. Given subsets $X,Y\subseteq S$, the \emph{symmetric difference} of $X$ and $Y$ is denoted by $X\triangle Y\coloneqq (X\setminus Y)\cup(Y\setminus X)$. If $Y$ consists of a single element $y$, then $X\setminus\{y\}$ and $X\cup \{y\}$ are abbreviated as $X-y$ and $X+y$, respectively. Given a weight $w\in \bR^S$, the weight of a set $F \in \cF$ is $w(F) \coloneqq \sum_{s \in F} w(s)$.  By convention, we define $\min \emptyset =+\infty$ and $\max \emptyset =-\infty$. Given a vector $p\in\bR^S$ and $1\leq q<\infty$, %its \emph{$\ell_q$-norm} is $\|p\|_q\coloneqq (\sum_{s\in S}|p(s)|^q )^{1/q}$,
its \emph{$\ell_\infty$-norm} is $\|p\|_\infty\coloneqq\sum_{s\in S}p(s)$. %, and its \emph{span} is $\spa(p)\coloneqq\max\{p(s)\mid s\in S\}-\min\{p(s)\mid s\in S\}$. 
The \emph{characteristic vector} of a set $Z\subseteq S$ is $\chi_Z(s)=1$ if $s\in Z$ and $0$ otherwise.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Matroids}
\label{sec:matroid}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Here we give a brief summary of the basic definitions from matroid theory; for further details, we refer the reader to~\cite{oxley2011matroid}.

A \emph{matroid} $M=(S,\cI)$ is defined by its \emph{ground set} $S$ and its \emph{family of independent sets} $\cI\subseteq 2^S$ that satisfies the \emph{independence axioms}: (I1) $\emptyset\in\cI$, (I2) $X\subseteq Y,\ Y\in\cI\Rightarrow X\in\cI$, and (I3) $X,Y\in\cI,\ |X|<|Y|\Rightarrow\exists e\in Y\setminus X\ s.t.\ X+e\in\cI$. The \emph{rank} $r(X)$ of a set $X$ is the maximum size of an independent set in $X$. The \emph{rank} of the matroid is $r(S)$. The maximal independent subsets of $S$ are called \emph{bases} and their family is usually denoted by $\cB$. If the matroid is given by its rank function or by its family of bases instead of its independent sets, then we write $M=(S,r)$ or $M=(S,\cB)$, respectively.

An inclusionwise minimal non-independent set is called a \emph{circuit}. A subset $X \subseteq S$ is said to \emph{span} or \emph{generate} a subset $Y \subseteq S$ if $r(X \cup Y) = r(X)$. The \emph{closure} of $X$ is defined as $\cl(X)=\{s\in S\mid r(X+s)=r(X)\}$. The closure of $X$ is also called the set \emph{generated} or \emph{spanned} by $X$. 

Let $M_1=(S_1,\cI_1),\dots,M_k=(S_k,\cI_k)$ be matroids on disjoint ground sets and let $S=S_1\cup\dots\cup S_k$. The {\it direct sum} $M_1\oplus \dots\oplus M_k$ is the matroid $M=(S,\cI)$, where $\cI=\{I\subseteq S\mid I\cap S_i\in\cI_i\ \text{for $i\in[k]$}\}$. For a matroid $M=(S,r)$, let $S=S_1\cup\dots\cup S_k$ be the partition of its ground set into the connected components of the circuit hypergraph of $M$. Then the matroids $M_i=M|S_i$ for $i\in[k]$ are called the {\it connected components} of $M$ and $M=M_1\oplus\dots\oplus M_k$. We say that $M$ is {\it connected} if $k=1$, or in other words, if for any two elements $x,y\in S$ there exists a circuit containing both of them. The connected components of $M$ can be computed in polynomial time by taking any basis $B$ and computing the connected components of the hypergraph of fundamental circuits $C(B, e)$ for $e \in S \setminus B$.

Given a matroid $M=(S,\cB)$, the \emph{dual of $M$} is the matroid $M^* = (S, \cB^*)$ where $\cB^* \coloneqq \{  X \mid S\setminus X \in \cB \}$, that is, the bases of $M^*$ are the complements of the bases of $M$. For a matroid $M=(S,\cI)$, its \emph{restriction} to a subset $S'\subseteq S$ is the matroid $M|{S'}=(S',\cI')$ where $\cI'=\{I\in\cI\mid I\subseteq S'\}$.

The following observation gives an extension of the exchange property, see e.g.~\cite[Theorem 5.3.4]{frank2011book}. 

\begin{prop}\label{prop:exchange}
For any two bases $B_1$ and $B_2$ of a matroid, there exists a bijection $\varphi\colon B_1\setminus B_2\to B_2\setminus B_1$ such that $B_1-e+\varphi(e)$ is a basis for every $e\in B_1\setminus B_2$.    
\end{prop}

A well-known characterization of a basis having maximum weight is as follows, see e.g.~\cite[Thm. 5.5.3]{frank2011book}.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:charact_optimum_basis}
    A basis $B$ is of maximum weight if and only if $w(f) \leq w(e)$ for every pair of elements $f \in S - B$ and $e \in C(B, f)$.
\end{prop}

As usual in matroid algorithms, we assume that the matroid is given by an independence oracle, and we measure the running time by the number of oracle calls and other elementary operations. For ease of discussion, by a ``polynomial number'' of oracle calls we mean polynomial in the size of the ground set of the matroid. 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{\textsc{Inverse Matroid}}
\label{sec:offline}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this section, we focus on \textsc{IM}, where the underlying optimization problem is to find a maximum-weight basis of a matroid. In Section~\ref{sec:offline_prep}, we recall some of the results presented in~\cite{berczi2023infinity} that we will rely on in our proofs. Section~\ref{sec:offline_infty_span} then presents our algorithm that is significantly simpler and faster than previous approaches. Furthermore, our technique leads to a refinement of the min-max theorem from~\cite{berczi2023infinity}. Formally, \textsc{IM} is defined as follows.

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid (\textsc{IM})}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a fixed basis $B^*$, a weight function $w\in\bR^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bR^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item \label{it:IM_a} $B^*$ is a maximum-weight basis of $M$ with respect to $w^*$, and
        \item \label{it:IM_c} $\|w-w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

Then a weight function is called \textit{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:IM_a} holds. Note that \textsc{IM} is always feasible: set $w^*(s)=0$ for all $s\in S$.

\begin{remark}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM}. To do so, compute a maximum $w'$-weight basis of $M$, and compare its weight to that of $B^*$.
\end{remark} 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Previous results}
\label{sec:offline_prep}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textsc{IO} under the $\ell_\infty$-norm was studied by Bérczi, Mendoza-Cadena, and Varga~\cite{berczi2023infinity}. A key step in their proof was showing that for the $\ei$-norm, \textsc{IO} always admits an optimal weight function $w^*$ with a special structure.

\begin{prop}[Bérczi, Mendoza-Cadena, and Varga~\cite{berczi2023infinity}]\label{prop:ell_infty_devVector_of_special_form}     
 Let $\delta^*$ be the optimum value of an \textsc{IO} instance $( S, \cF, F^*, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$. Then, for any $\delta\geq \delta^*$, 
    \begin{equation*} 
        w_\delta(s)\coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
        w(s) + \delta  & \text{if $s \in F^*$}, \\
        w(s) - \delta  & \text{if $s \in S\setminus F^*$}
        \end{cases} 
    \end{equation*}
    is a feasible weight function. In particular, $w_{\delta^*}$ is an optimal solution.
\end{prop}

Furthermore, using this observation, the following min-max characterization was also provided in~\cite{berczi2023infinity}. 

\begin{prop}[Bérczi, Mendoza-Cadena, and Varga~\cite{berczi2023infinity}]\label{prop:min-max_offline_infinity}
    Consider an instance $( S, \cF, F^*, w,\|\cdot\|_\infty )$ of \textsc{IO}. Then
    \begin{align*}
        &\min \big\{ \| w-w' \|_\infty \bigm| w' \text{ is a feasible weight function} \big\} \\
        = &\max \biggl\{ 0, \max \Bigl\{  \frac{ w(F) - w(F^*)}{|F \triangle F^*|} \Bigm| F \in \cF\setminus\{F^*\} \Bigr\}  \biggr\}.
    \end{align*}
\end{prop}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm and min-max theorem}
\label{sec:offline_infty_span}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The goal of this section is to design an algorithm for \textsc{IM} and to prove a refined version of Proposition~\ref{prop:min-max_offline_infinity} for \textsc{IM}. Our algorithm requires a single maximum-weight basis computation, improving upon the $O(|S|)$ calls required by the algorithm in~\cite{berczi2023infinity}, and being simpler and faster than those of~\cite{mao1999inverse,Ahuja2001Inverse,zhang2002general,zhang1999further, li2016algorithm,zhang2016partialmatroid}.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:im}
Let $B_{\max}$ be a maximum $w$-weight basis of $M$, and let $B^*$ be a fixed basis. If $w(B_{\max}) > w(B^*)$, then there exist elements $f \in B_{\max}$ and $e \in B^*$ with $w(f) > w(e)$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]\itemsep0em
    \item $\delta^* =  (w(f) - w(e)) / 2$ is the optimum value of the IM instance $(M=(S,\cI),B^*,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$, and \label{part:1}
    \item $B' =  B^* + f - e$ is a basis attaining the maximum in Proposition~\ref{prop:ell_infty_devVector_of_special_form}. \label{part:2}
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if both $B_{\max}$ and $B^*$ are given, then the value of $\delta^*$ can be found using $O(r\cdot \log r)$ elementary operations and independence oracle calls, where $r$ denotes the rank of $M$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Label $B^*=\{e_1,\dots,e_r\}$ so that $w(e_1)\ge\dots\ge w(e_r)$.  For each $f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*$, we define $\ind(f)=\min\{i\mid f\in\cl\{e_1,\dots,e_i\}\}$ and $\gamma_f=\max\{w(f)-w(e_{\ind(f)}),0\}$. Finally, let $\delta^*=\max\{\gamma_f/2\mid f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*\}$.

We claim that $\delta^*$ is the optimum value of the \textsc{IM} instance considered. To prove this, define a modified weight function $w^*$ as in Proposition~\ref{prop:ell_infty_devVector_of_special_form}, namely
\[
w^*(s)=
\begin{cases}
w(s)+\delta^* & \text{if $s\in B^*$},\\
w(s)-\delta^* & \text{if $s\notin B^*$}.
\end{cases}
\]
We define a total order $s_1, \dots, s_n$ on the ground set $S$, sorted in decreasing order of $w^*$-value. To break ties, elements in $B^*$ come before those in $B_{\max} \setminus B^*$, which in turn come before elements in $S \setminus (B^* \cup B_{\max})$. That is, if $w^*(s_i) = w^*(s_j)$, then $s_i$ precedes $s_j$ whenever $s_i \in B^*$ and $s_j \notin B^*$, or when $s_i \in B_{\max}$ and $s_j \notin B^* \cup B_{\max}$. Ties not resolved by these rules are broken using any fixed total order on $S$.

We claim that the greedy algorithm applied to $S$ under this order returns $B_G = B^*$. To see this, note that since $B_{\max}$ is a maximum $w$-weight basis, any element $g \in S \setminus (B_{\max} \cup B^*)$ satisfies
\[
g \in \cl\bigl(\{f \in B_{\max} \mid w(f) \geq w(g)\}\bigr) \subseteq \operatorname{cl}\bigl(\{s_j \in S \mid j < i\}\bigr),
\]
where $s_i = g$ is its position in the ordering. Hence, $g\notin B_G$. Furthermore, for every $f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*$,
\[
f\in \cl\bigl(\{e_1,\dots,e_{\ind(f)}\}\bigr) \subseteq \cl\bigl(\{e\in B^*\mid w(e)\geq w(f)-\gamma_f\}\bigr)   \subseteq \cl\bigl(\{s_j \in S \mid j < i\}\bigr),
\]
where $s_i = f$ is its position in the ordering. Hence, $f \notin B_G$. Therefore, no element outside $B^*$ is selected by the greedy algorithm, which implies that $B_G \subseteq B^*$. Since $B_G$ and $B^*$ are both bases, it follows that $B_G = B^*$.

This shows that $w^*$ is feasible for \textsc{IM} on the instance $(M=(S,\cI),B^*,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$, and hence the optimal value is at most $\delta^*$. To show that it cannot be strictly smaller, suppose that we set $\delta^*< \max\{\gamma_f/2\mid f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*\}$. This implies that there exists $f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*$ with $2\cdot \delta^*<\gamma_f$. Let $e=e_{\ind(f)}\in B^*$. By the basis exchange property, $B^*+f-e$ is also a basis and
\begin{align*}
w^*(B^*+f-e)
&=
w^*(B^*)+w^*(f)-w^*(e)\\
&=
w^*(B^*)+(w(f)-w(e))-2\cdot \delta^*\\
&= 
w^*(B^*)+\gamma_f -2\cdot \delta^*\\
&> 
w^*(B^*),
\end{align*}
showing that $w^*$ is not feasible for smaller values of $\delta^*$.

The above proof is algorithmic. We start by sorting $B^*=\{e_1,\dots, e_r\}$ according to $w$ in a decreasing order. For each $f\in B_{\max}\setminus B^*$, we can compute $\ind(f)$ in $O(\log r)$ oracle calls by testing independence over sets of the form $\{e_1,\dots, e_s\}$ using binary search over $s$. In total, this gives $O(r\cdot \log r)$ oracle calls and elementary operations.
\end{proof}

As a corollary, we get that for \textsc{IM}, the maximum in Proposition~\ref{prop:min-max_offline_infinity} is attained on a basis that is close to the fixed basis $B^*$ in terms of symmetric difference, thus giving a refinement of the min-max result in~\cite{berczi2023infinity} in the matroidal setting. We also read out a similar statement that we will use later on.

\begin{cor} \label{cor:IM-offline-infty_symmDiff_equals_2}
    Let $M=(S,\cB)$ be a matroid, $B^*\in\cB$ be a fixed basis, and $w\in\bR^S$ be a weight function.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]\itemsep0em
        \item If $B^*$ is not of maximum $w$-weight, then there exists $B' \in \arg\max \big\{  \frac{w(B) - w(B^*)}{|B \triangle B^*|} \big| B \in \cB  \big\}$ such that $| B' \triangle B^*| = 2$. \label{it:a}
        \item If $B^*$ is not of minimum $w$-weight, then there exists $B' \in \arg\min \big\{  \frac{w(B^*) - w(B)}{|B^* \triangle B|} \big| B \in \cB  \big\}$ such that $| B^* \triangle B'| = 2$. \label{it:b}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
Part~\ref{it:a} is a direct consequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:im}, and~\ref{it:b} follows from~\ref{it:a} by multiplying $w$ by $-1$.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{\textsc{Inverse Matroid Exists}}\label{sec:IM-Exists}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

From now on, we introduce several extensions of \textsc{IM} in which, instead of a fixed basis $B^*$, a subset $S_0$ is given, and various constraints are imposed on the set of optimal solutions depending on $S_0$. Our first goal is to find a weight function $w^*$ close to $w$ that ensures $S_0$ contains a maximum $w^*$-weight basis. 

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid Exists (IM-Exists)}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that contains a basis, a weight function $w\in\bR^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bR^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item \label{it:feas_cond_IM-Exists} there exists a basis contained in $S_0$ that has maximum $w^*$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:IM_c_IM-Exists} $\|w-w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

Similarly as in \textsc{IM}, we say that a weight function is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_IM-Exists} holds. Note that \textsc{IM-Exists} is always feasible: set $w^*(s)=0$ for all $s \in S$.

\begin{remark}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-Exists}. To do so, it is enough to find a maximum $w'$-weight basis $B_0$ of $M|S_0$ and a maximum $w'$-weight basis $A$ of $M$. Then, $w'$ is feasible if and only if $w'(B_0) = w'(A)$.
\end{remark}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preparations}
\label{sec:prepexists}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

First, we give a characterization of feasible weight functions. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:IM-Exists_closure_for_feas_weight}
    A weight function $w'$ is feasible for an \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ if and only if $e \in  \cl( \{ f \in S_0 \mid w'(e) \leq w'(f)\} )$ for all $e \in S\setminus S_0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
   Suppose that $w'$ is feasible and that $B_0 \subseteq S_0$ is a basis of maximum $w'$-weight. Then, by running the greedy algorithm with weight function $w'$, while breaking ties in favour of elements in $S_0$, no element $e \in S \setminus S_0$ is selected, since the algorithm outputs $B_0$. This means that each $e \in S \setminus S_0$ is spanned by the set $\{ f \in B_0 \mid w'(e) \leq w'(f) \}$. Therefore, $e \in \cl\left( \{ f \in S_0 \mid w'(e) \leq w'(f) \} \right)$, as claimed.

    To see the other direction, assume that $e \in \cl(\{ f \in S_0 \mid w'(e) \leq w'(f)\})$ holds for all $e \in S \setminus S_0$. Then the greedy algorithm for weight function $w'$, where ties are broken by giving priority to elements in $S_0$ and arbitrarily otherwise, returns a basis of maximum $w'$-weight contained in $S_0$. Indeed, for every $e \in S \setminus S_0$, the set of elements considered before $e$ -- that is, those with weight greater than or equal to that of $e$ -- already generate $e$. This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

Similarly to \textsc{IM}, \textsc{IM-Exists} admits an optimal weight function $w^*$ that has a special structure.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:IM-Exists_special_weight}
    Let $\delta^*$ be the optimum value of an \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$. Then  
    \begin{equation*}
        w^*(s) = \begin{cases}
            w(s) +\delta^* &  \text{if $s \in S_0$}, \\
            w(s) -\delta^* & \text{if $s \in S \setminus S_0$}
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
     is an optimal weight function.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
As $\|w-w^*\|_\infty = \delta^*$, it suffices to show that $w^*$ is feasible. Let $w^{\text{opt}}$ be an optimal weight function. By Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_closure_for_feas_weight}, we know that $e \in \cl(\{ f \in S_0 \mid w^{\text{opt}}(e) \leq w^{\text{opt}}(f)\}) \subseteq \cl(\{ f \in S_0 \mid w^*(e) \leq w^*(f)\})$ for all $e \in S\setminus S_0$, since $w^*$ increases the weight of each element in $S_0$ and decreases the weight of each element in $S\setminus S_0$ at least as much as $w^{\text{opt}}$. Therefore, by Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_closure_for_feas_weight}, $w^*$ is also feasible.
\end{proof}

Let $w^*$ be an optimal solution for an \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ and $B_0\subseteq S_0$ be a maximum $w^*$-weight basis of $M$. Then, by Proposition~\ref{prop:min-max_offline_infinity} and Corollary~\ref{cor:IM-offline-infty_symmDiff_equals_2}\ref{it:a}, either $B_0$ has maximum $w$-weight in $M$, or $\|w-w^*\|=\arg\max \big\{  \frac{w(B) - w(B_0)}{2} \big| B \in \cB,|B \triangle B_0|=2 \big\}$. However, at this point it is not clear whether $B$ attaining the maximum is a subset of $S_0$ or not. Next we show that the element in $B\setminus B_0$ can be assumed to be in $S \setminus S_0$. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:IM-Exists_matroid_infty_2delta}
    Let $w^*$ be an optimal solution for an \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ and let $\delta^*=\| w - w^* \|_\infty$. Then either $\delta^*=0$, or there exists a triple $(B_0,e,f)$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]\itemsep0em
        \item\label{item:triple_prop1} $B_0 \subseteq S_0$ is a basis,
        \item\label{item:triple_prop2} $f \in S \setminus S_0$,
        \item\label{item:triple_prop3} $e \in S_0$, $e \in C(B_0,f) $ , and
        \item \label{item:triple_prop4}$w(f) - w(e) = 2\delta^*$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Assume that $\delta^*>0$. We call a triple $(B_0,e,f)$ is \emph{valid} if conditions \ref{item:triple_prop1} -- \ref{item:triple_prop3} hold. Note that a valid triple exists: $S_0$ contains a basis $B_0$ by assumption and $\delta^*>0$ implies $S\setminus S_0\neq\emptyset$, therefore for any $f\in S\setminus S_0$ and $e\in C(B_0,f)$ the triple $(B_0,e,f)$ is valid. 

    Suppose indirectly that $w(f) - w(e) < 2\delta^*$ for all valid triples $(B_0,e,f)$. Let $\delta' = \max \{ \frac{w(f) - w(e)}{2} \mid (B_0,e,f) \text{ is a valid triple} \}$, and let $(B_0,e,f)$ be a maximizer of $\delta'$.
    Consider the weight function    
    \begin{equation*}
        w'(s) = \begin{cases}
            w+\delta' &  \text{if $s \in B_0$}, \\
            w-\delta' & \text{if $s \in S \setminus B_0$}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}    
    We claim that $w'(B) \leq w'(B_0)$ for all basis $B \not \subseteq S_0$. To see this, let us denote $B_0 \setminus B = \{e_1, \dots, e_k \}$ and $B \setminus B_0 = \{f_1, \dots, f_k \}$, where $B_0-e_i + f_i$ is a basis of $M$ for all $i\in[k]$ -- such orderings exist by Proposition~\ref{prop:exchange}. By definition of $\delta'$, we have $w(f_i) - w(e_i) \leq w(f) - w(e) = 2\delta'$ for all $i \in [k]$. Thus,
    \begin{align*}
        w'(B)-w'(B_0)
        &=
        w(B)+\delta'\cdot |B\cap B_0|-\delta'\cdot |B\setminus B_0|-w(B_0)-\delta'\cdot |B_0|\\
        &= 
        w(B)-w(B_0)-\delta'\cdot |B\triangle B_0|\\
        &= 
        \sum_{i = 1 }^{k} \big(w(f_i) - w(e_i)-2\cdot \delta'\big)\\
        &\leq 
        0
    \end{align*}
    as stated. Thus $w'$ is a feasible weight function with $\|w-w'\|_\infty=\delta' < \delta^*=\|w-w^*\|_\infty$, contradicting the optimality of $w^*$. 
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm based on pairs}
\label{sec:algpairs}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

With the help of Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_matroid_infty_2delta}, we first give a simple algorithm for solving \textsc{IM-Exists}. The algorithm is presented as Algorithm~\ref{alg:binary}. 

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
\caption{Binary search algorithm for \textsc{IM-Exists}\label{alg:binary}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $(M=(S,\mathcal{I}),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ of \textsc{IM-Exists} where $S_0$ contains at least one basis.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight function $w^*$.}
    Let $L$ be the list of values $(w(e) - w(f))/2$ for all $e\in S_0$ and $f\in S \setminus S_0$ in an increasing order.\;
    Set $\ell \coloneqq 1$, $u\coloneqq|L|$.\;
    \While{$\ell \neq u $}{
        Set $\delta$ as the $\lfloor (\ell + u)/2  \rfloor$-th element of the list $L$.\;
        Set $w^*(s) \coloneqq w(s) +\delta$ if $s \in S_0$ and $w^*(s)\coloneqq w(s) -\delta$ if $s \in S \setminus S_0$.\;  
        Let $B_0$ and $B$ be maximum $w^*$-weight bases of $M|S_0$ and $M$, respectively.\;
        \If{$w^*(B_0)\geq w^*(B)$}{
            Update $u\gets \lfloor (\ell + u)/2  \rfloor$.\;
            }
        \Else{ 
            Update $\ell\gets \lceil (\ell + u)/2  \rceil$. \;
            }   
        }
    \Return{$w^*$}\;
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:IM-Exists_binarySearch}
    Algorithm~\ref{alg:binary} determines an optimal weight function $w^*$ for the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    The correctness of the algorithm follows by Proposition~\ref{prop:min-max_offline_infinity}, Corollary~\ref{cor:IM-offline-infty_symmDiff_equals_2}\ref{it:a} and Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_matroid_infty_2delta}. Each step of the algorithm can be performed in polynomial time, thus the theorem follows.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Reduction to \textsc{IM}}
\label{sec:algred}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

While Algorithm~\ref{alg:binary} is straightforward, it does not clarify whether there exists some stronger relationship between \textsc{IM} and \textsc{IM-Exists}. In what follows, we describe a different algorithm that reduces the latter to the former. The algorithm is presented as Algorithm~\ref{algo:problem4_opt_devVector} 

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
\caption{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Exists} via reduction to \textsc{IM}\label{algo:problem4_opt_devVector}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $(M=(S,\mathcal{I}),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ of \textsc{IM-Exists} where $S_0$ contains at least one basis.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight function $w^*$.}
    Let $B_0$ be a maximum $w$-weight basis of $M|S_0$. \;
    Let $w^*$ be an optimal solution for \textsc{IM} instance $(M=(S,\cI),B_0,w, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$.\;
    \Return{$w^*$}\;
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:IM-Exists-combAlgo}
    Algorithm~\ref{algo:problem4_opt_devVector} determines an optimal weight function $w^*$ for the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    Consider an optimal weight function $w^*$ provided by Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_special_weight} with $\|w-w^*\|_\infty=\delta^*$. Let $B$ and $B_0$ be maximum $w^*$- and $w$-weight bases contained in $S_0$, respectively. Then 
\begin{equation*}
    w^*(B) = w(B) + \delta^* \cdot |B| \leq w(B_0) + \delta^* \cdot |B_0| =  w^*(B_0),
\end{equation*}
hence $B_0$ is also a maximum $w^*$-weight basis contained in $S_0$. Let $w'$ denote an optimal solution for the \textsc{IM} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  B_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ and let $\delta'=\|w-w'\|_\infty$; clearly, $\delta' \leq \delta^*$. On the other hand, $\delta^*$ can be seen as the minimum value among all $\delta$'s that make at least one basis $B \subseteq S_0$ to have maximum weight, and thus $\delta^* \leq  \delta'$. This implies that $w'$ is also an optimal solution for the original \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$. The algorithm computes exactly such a weight function, concluding the proof.
\end{proof}

Note that the running time of Algorithm~\ref{algo:problem4_opt_devVector} is essentially determined by that of $\cO$; in other words, \textsc{IM-Exists} admits an algorithm with the same time complexity as \textsc{IM}.

\begin{remark}
    If $w\in\bZ^S$ and $w^*$ is required to be integer-valued, then an analogous approach works: it suffices to take the ceiling of $w^*(s)$ for all $s \in S$.
\end{remark}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{\textsc{Inverse Matroid All} and \textsc{Only}}
\label{sec:IM-All}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this section we consider two extensions of \textsc{IM} with constraints on all bases contained in $S_0$. In the first one, the goal is to find a weight function that makes all the bases contained in $S_0$ to be of maximum weight.

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid All (\textsc{IM-All})}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$, a weight function $w\in\bR^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bR^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item{\label{it:feas_cond_IM-All}} all bases contained in $S_0$ have maximum $w^*$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:IM_c_IM-All} $\|p\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

We say that a weight function is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_IM-All} holds.  Note that \textsc{IM-All} is always feasible, even if $S_0$ does not contain a basis of $M$: set $w^*(s)=0$ for all $s \in S$.

\begin{remark}\label{rem:feasibility_check_IM-All}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-All}. To do so, it is enough to find a minimum $w'$-weight basis $B^1_0$ of $M|S_0$,  a maximum $w'$-weight basis $B^2_0$ of $M|S_0$, and a maximum $w'$-weight basis $A$ of $M$. Then, $w'$ is feasible if and only if $w'(B^2_0)=2'(B^1_0)\geq w'(A)$.
\end{remark}

In the second variant, $S_0$ is required to contain exactly the bases of maximum weight. Ahmadian, Bhaskar, Sanit{\`a}, and Swamy~\cite{ahmadian2018algorithms} proposed an LP-based approach for a related problem, where the goal is to find a weight function rather than modify one. Our problem differs from previous ones in that an optimal solution does not necessarily exist. Indeed, for a given cost function, it may happen that every basis contained in $S_0$ has maximum weight, yet there also exists a maximum-weight basis not contained in $S_0$. In such cases, by increasing the weights of elements in $S_0$ by an arbitrarily small value $\varepsilon$ and decreasing the weights of elements outside $S_0$ accordingly, one can obtain a feasible solution. To avoid this, we consider integer-valued weight functions.

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid Only (\textsc{IM-Only})}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that contains at least one basis, a weight function $w\in\bZ^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bZ^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item{\label{it:feas_cond_IM-Only}} the bases contained in $S_0$ are exactly the ones of maximum $w^*$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:IM_c_IM-Only} $\|w- w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

We say that a weight function is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_IM-Only} holds. Note that \textsc{IM-Only} is always feasible: set $w^*(s)=1$ for all $s\in S_0$ and $w^*(s)=0$ for all $s\in S\setminus S_0$.

\begin{remark}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-Only}. First find a minimum $w'$-weight basis $B_0^1$ of $M|S_0$ and a maximum $w'$-weight basis $B_0^2$ of $M|S_0$, and check if $w'(B_0^2)=w'(B_0^1)$. If no, then $w'$ is not feasible. Otherwise, run the greedy algorithm while breaking ties in favour of elements in $S\setminus S_0$. If the resulting basis is not contained in $S_0$, then $w'$ is not feasible, otherwise it is feasible.
\end{remark} 


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preparations}
\label{sec:prepall}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

One of the key tools in solving \textsc{IM-Exists} was Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_special_weight}, which enabled the search for an optimal weight function in a special form. Unfortunately, \textsc{IM-All} and \textsc{IM-Only} do not necessarily admit an optimal solution with such a simple structure, which results in more involved algorithms. Our approach relies on the folklore result that if all bases of a matroid have the same weight, then the weight function must be constant on its connected components. For completeness, we include a proof to make the paper self-contained.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:connected_components_decomp}
    Let $M = (S, \cI)$ be a matroid and $w \in \mathbb{R}^S$ be a weight function. Then, every basis has the same $w$-weight if and only if $w$ is constant on the connected components of $M$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $S=S_1\cup\dots\cup S_k$ be the partition of the ground set into the connected components of the circuit hypergraph of $M$, and let $M_i=M|S_i$ for $i\in[k]$. Since $M=M_1\oplus\dots\oplus M_k$, we get that $B\subseteq S$ is a basis of $M$ if and only if $B_i=B\cap S_i$ is a basis of $M_i$ for all $i\in[k]$.

    Assume first that $w$ is a constant on every $S_i$. Then, every basis of $M_i$ has the same weight, and so the same holds for their direct sum, proving the `if' direction.

    Now suppose that there exists $i\in[k]$ and $e,f\in S_i$ such that $w(e)\neq w(f)$; we may assume without loss of generality that $i=1$. Since $e$ and $f$ are in the same component of $M$, there exists a circuit $C$ containing both of them. Let $B$ be a basis of $M$ containing $C-f$. Then $C(B,f)=C$, and so $B'=B-e+f$ is a basis with $w(B')\neq w(B)$, proving the `only if' direction. 
\end{proof}

Another simple but useful observation is the following. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hm}
Let $w_\textsc{Feas}$ be a feasible solution for the \textsc{IM-All} or \textsc{IM-Only} instance $(M=(S,\cI),S_0,w,\allowbreak \|\cdot\|_\infty)$, and let $\delta=\|w-w_\textsc{Feas}\|_\infty$. Then there exists a feasible solution $w'_\textsc{Feas}$ satisfying the following:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]\itemsep0em
    \item $\|w-w'_\textsc{Feas}\|_\infty=\delta$, \label{prop:a}
    \item for every component $M_i=(S_i,\cI_i)$ of $M|S_0$, there exists $e\in S_i$ with $w'_\textsc{Feas}(e)-w(e)=\delta$, and \label{prop:b}
    \item $w'_\textsc{Feas}(e)-w(e)=-\delta$ for all $e\in S\setminus S_0$. \label{prop:c}
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    If $w_\textsc{Feas}(e)-w(e)<\delta$ for all $e\in S_i$, then we can further increase the weights on $S_i$ by $\min\{\delta-(w_\textsc{Feas}(e)-w(e))\mid e\in S_i\}$ without changing the value of $\|w-w_\textsc{Feas}\|_\infty$, and such a step also preserves the feasibility of the weight function. 

    For the second part, observe that it is worth decreasing the weights as much as possible on elements outside $S_0$.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-All}}
\label{sec:algall}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Our algorithm for \textsc{IM-All} relies on Proposition~\ref{prop:charact_optimum_basis} and Lemma~\ref{lem:connected_components_decomp}. These results imply that for any basis $B_0 \subseteq S_0$, a feasible weight function $w'$ must satisfy $w'(y) = w'(x)$ for all $y \in S_0 \setminus B_0$ and $x \in C(B_0,y)$, and $w'(y) \leq w(x)$ for all $y \in S \setminus S_0$ and $x \in C(B_0,y)$. The high-level idea of the algorithm is as follows.

\paragraph{Phase 1: Homogenization.} 
First, we determine the connected components $M_1 = (S_1, \cI_1), \dots, M_k = (S_k, \cI_k)$ of $M|S_0$. Then, for each component $M_i$, we modify the weights so that every element in $S_i$ has the same weight. Since these modifications may differ across components, we identify the change with the largest $\ell_\infty$-distance from the original weights, and apply an additional shift to all components so that they are adjusted by the same amount. We denote the resulting weight function by $w_H$.

\paragraph{Phase 2: Maximization.} 
We check whether $w_H$ is feasible. If it is not, we compute the optimal value $\Delta$ of the \textsc{IM} instance $(M, B_0, w_H, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$ for some basis $B_0 \subseteq S_0$. We then define the weight function $w^*$ by setting $w^*(s) = w_H(s) + \Delta$ for all $s \in S_0$ and $w^*(s) = w_H(s) - \Delta$ for all $s \in S \setminus S_0$.\\    

The formal description of the algorithm is given as Algorithm~\ref{alg:IM-All}; see Figure~\ref{fig:example_IM-All} for an illustration.

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
\caption{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-All}\label{alg:IM-All}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $(M=(S,\mathcal{I}),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ of \textsc{IM-All}.}%, and an oracle $\cO$ for instances $(M=(S,\mathcal{I}), B_0, w,\| \cdot \|_\infty)$ of \textsc{IM} where $B_0\subseteq S_0$.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight function $w^*$.}
    \If{$M$ has no basis contained in $S_0$}{
        Set $w^* \coloneqq w$.\;        
    }
    \Else{
    \tcp*[l]{Phase 1: Homogenization}
        Determine the connected components $M_1 = (S_1, \cI_1), \dots, M_k = (S_k, \cI_k)$ of $M|S_0$.\;  
        \For{$i \in [k]$}{
        Set $\delta_i^{\max}  \coloneqq \max \{ w(e) \mid e \in S_i \}$ and  $\delta_i^{\min}  =\min \{ w(e) \mid e \in S_i \} $. \;
        Set $m_i  \coloneqq (\delta_i^{\max} + \delta_i^{\min})/2$.\;
        }
        Set $\varrho \coloneqq \max_{i \in [k]} \{ w(e_i^{\max}) - m_i\}$.\label{step:rho}\;
        Set $\operatorname{shift}_i \coloneqq \varrho - \left( \delta_i^{\max} - m_i \right)$.\;
        Set $w_H(s)\coloneqq m_i + \operatorname{shift}_i$ if $s \in S_0$ and $w_H(s)\coloneqq w(s) - \varrho$ if $s \in S \setminus S_0$.\;
    \tcp*[l]{Phase 2: Maximization}
        Let $\Delta$ be the optimum value of the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $(M=(S, \cI), S_0, w_H, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$.\;
        Set $w^*(s)\coloneqq w_H(s) + \Delta$ if $s \in S_0$ and $w^*(s)\coloneqq w_H(s) - \Delta$ if $s \in S \setminus S_0$.\;
    }
    \Return{$w^*$.}\;
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:IM-All_algorithm}
    Algorithm~\ref{alg:IM-All} determines an optimal weight function $w^*$ for the \textsc{IM-All} instance $(M=(S,\cI),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    The algorithm outputs a weight function $w^*$ that satisfies the conditions of Proposition~\ref{prop:charact_optimum_basis} and Lemma~\ref{lem:connected_components_decomp}, and is therefore feasible. Since $\varrho=\min\{\|w-w'\|_\infty\mid \text{every basis in $S_0$ has the same $w'$-weight}\}$, $\delta\geq \varrho$ necessarily holds.

    To prove optimality, let $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$ be an optimal solution that satisfies conditions~\ref{prop:a}--\ref{prop:c} of Lemma~\ref{lem:hm}, and let $\delta=\|w-w^*_{\textsc{All}}\|_\infty$. Then we get $w_H(s)=w^*_{\textsc{All}}(s)-\delta+\varrho$ for all $s\in S_0$ and $w_H(s)=w^*_{\textsc{All}}(s)+\delta-\varrho$ for all $s\in S\setminus S_0$. That is, $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$ is obtained from the homogenized weight function $w_H$ by increasing and decreasing the weights on $S_0$ and $S\setminus S_0$, respectively, by the same amount. Nevertheless, the algorithm does exactly the same using the optimum value, proving optimality.

    As mentioned earlier in Section~\ref{sec:matroid}, the connected components of $M|S_0$ can be determined in polynomial time by computing the connected components of the hypergraph of fundamental circuits for elements in $S_0 \setminus B_0$, with respect to an arbitrary basis $B_0$ of $M|S_0$. Computing the value of $\Delta$ requires solving an \textsc{IM} instance, which also takes polynomial time.
\end{proof}

