\subsection{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Only}}
\label{sec:algonly}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We show that \textsc{IM-Only} can be solved by first computing an optimal weight function for the corresponding \textsc{IM-All} instance, and then slightly modifying it, if needed, so that the bases in $S_0$ are exactly the set of maximizers. 

\begin{thm}\label{thm:IM-Only_algorithm}
    Let $w^*_{\textsc{All}} \in \mathbb{Z}^S$ be an optimal solution to the \textsc{IM-All} instance $(M = (S, \mathcal{I}), S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty)$ under integrality constraints, and suppose it satisfies conditions~\ref{prop:a}--\ref{prop:c} of Lemma~\ref{lem:hm}. Then either $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$ or $w^*_{\textsc{All}} + \chi_{S_0} - \chi_{S \setminus S_0}$ is an optimal solution to the \textsc{IM-Only} problem for the same instance.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    Let $w^*_{\textsc{Only}}$ be an optimal weight function for the \textsc{IM-Only} instance $(M = (S, \mathcal{I}), S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty)$ and let $\delta=\|w-w^*_{\textsc{Only}}\|_\infty$. Since $w^*_{\textsc{Only}}$ is also a feasible solution for the \textsc{IM-All} problem on the same instance, we may assume that it satisfies conditions~\ref{prop:a}--\ref{prop:c} of Lemma~\ref{lem:hm}.

    First, we show that if $\|w - w^*_{\textsc{Only}}\|_\infty=\|w - w^*_{\textsc{All}}\|_\infty$, then $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-Only}, and hence it is optimal. This follows immediately from conditions~\ref{prop:b} and~\ref{prop:c}, along with the fact that both $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$ and $w^*_{\textsc{Only}}$ are constant on the connected components of $M|S_0$. Together with the equality $\|w - w^*_{\textsc{All}}\|_\infty = \|w - w^*_{\textsc{Only}}\|_\infty$, this implies that the two weight functions in fact coincide.

    Otherwise $\|w - w^*_{\textsc{Only}}\|_\infty\geq \|w - w^*_{\textsc{All}}\|_\infty \|+1$ by the integrality constraints. Consider the weight function
    \begin{align*}
        w'(s) = \begin{cases}
            w^*_{\textsc{All}}(s) + 1 & \text{if } s\in S_0, \\
            w^*_{\textsc{All}}(s) - 1 & \text{if } s\in S \setminus S_0.
        \end{cases}        
    \end{align*}
    Since every basis contained in $S_0$ has maximum $w^*_{\textsc{All}}$-weight, the same holds under $w'$. Moreover, for any pair of bases $B \not\subseteq S_0$ and $B_0 \subseteq S_0$, we have $w'(B_0)=w_{\textsc{All}}(B_0)+|B_0|>w_{\textsc{All}}(B)+|B\cap S_0|=w'(B)$. Thus, $w'$ is a feasible solution for \textsc{IM-Only} with $\|w-w'\|_\infty=\|w-w^*_\textsc{All}\|_\infty+1$, and therefore it is also optimal.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{\textsc{Inverse Matroid Not Exists}}
\label{sec:not-IM-Exists}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We turn to the negated versions of the problems studied in Sections~\ref{sec:IM-Exists} and \ref{sec:IM-All}, starting with \textsc{Inverse Matroid Exists}. Recall that in the negated variant, \textsc{Inverse Matroid Not Exists}, the goal is to modify the weight function as little as possible so that $S_0$ contains no maximum weight basis of $M$ with respect to the modified weights. A similar problem was considered in~\cite{li2016algorithm,zhang2016partialmatroid} where $S_0$ is an independent set, the weight function can be only increased or only decreased, and the objective is to minimize $\|w-w^*\|_\infty$. In contrast, we consider arbitrary subsets $S_0$ of $S$, and permit both increases and decreases to the weights. For similar reasons as in the case of \textsc{IM-Only}, we consider integer-valued weight functions. 

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid Not Exists (\textsc{IM-Not-Exists})}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that does not contain some basis, a weight function $w\in\bZ^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bZ^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item \label{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Exists} none of the bases contained in $S_0$ is of maximum $w^*$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:opt_cond_not-IM-Exists} $\| w - w^* \|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

We say that a weight function $w$ is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Exists} holds. Note that \textsc{IM-Not-Exists} is always feasible: set $w'$ such that $w'(s)=0$ for all $s \in S_0$ and $w'(s)=1$ for all $s \in S \setminus S_0$.

\begin{remark}
    Given a weight $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}. To do so, run the greedy algorithm while breaking ties in favour of elements in $S_0$. Then $w'$ is feasible if and only if the resulting basis is not contained in $S_0$.
\end{remark}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Base cases}
\label{sec:easy}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The problem has two easy cases. Clearly, if $S_0$ does not contain a maximum $w$-weight basis, then we are done. The second is stated below.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Not-IM-Exists_easy_case}
    Let $(M=(S,\cI),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$ be an instance of \textsc{IM-Not-Exists} and assume that $M$ has a maximum $w$-weight basis contained in $S_0$, and also one not contained in $S_0$. Then $w^*=w-\chi_{S_0}+\chi_{S\setminus S_0}$ is an optimal solution for the problem.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    By the assumption of the theorem and the integrality constraints, we know that the optimum value of any feasible solution is at least $1$. Since $\|w-w^*\|_\infty=1$, it suffices to show that $w^*$ is feasible. To see this, let $B_0\subseteq S_0$ be an arbitrary basis of $M$, and let $B_{\max}$ be a maximum $w$-weight basis of $M$ not contained in $S_0$. Then
    \begin{align*}
        w^*(B_0)
        & = w(B_0) - |B_0| \\
        & \leq w(B_{\max}) - |B_{\max}| \\
        & < w(B_{\max}) - |B_{\max}\cap S_0| + |B_{\max}\setminus S_0|\\
        & = w^*(B_{\max}),
    \end{align*}
concluding the proof.  
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Relaxed Version}
\label{sec:relaxed-not-IM-Exists}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

When every maximum $w$-weight basis of $M$ is contained in $S_0$, additional steps are required. To address this, we define a relaxed version of the problem, which allows a maximum-weight basis to lie within $S_0$ and does not require the weight function to be integral.

\problemdef{Relaxed Inverse Matroid Not Exists (Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists)}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that does not contain some basis, a weight function $w\in\bR^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $\widehat{w} \in \bR^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item \label{it:feas_cond_relaxed} there exists a basis not contained in $S_0$ that is of maximum $\widehat{w}$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:opt_cond_relaxed} $\|w-\widehat{w}\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

We say that a weight function $w$ is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_relaxed} holds. Note that \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} is always feasible: set $w'$ such that $w'(s)=0$ for all $s \in S_0$ and $w'(s)=1$ for all $s \in S \setminus S_0$.

\begin{remark}\label{rem:relaxedProb_feasibility}
    Given a weight $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists}. To do so, run the greedy algorithm while breaking ties in favour of elements in $S\setminus S_0$. Then $w'$ is feasible if and only if the resulting basis is not contained in $S_0$.
\end{remark}

We first show that \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} again admit an optimal weight function that has a special form.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:relaxedProb_Nicer_special_weight_2}
    Let $\widehat{\delta}$ be the optimum value of an \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$. Then  
    \begin{equation}
        w'(s) = \begin{cases}
            w(s) -\widehat{\delta} &  s \in S_0 , \\
            w(s) +\widehat{\delta} & s \in S \setminus S_0
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
     is an optimal weight function. Furthermore, if $w$ is integer-valued, then $\widehat{\delta} = (w(f)-w(e))/2$ for some pair $e \in S_0$ and $f \in S \setminus S_0$ and so it is half-integral.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Assume that $\widehat{w}$ is an optimal solution to the \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance, and let $B$ be a maximum $\widehat{w}$-weight basis not contained in $S_0$. Let $x \in B \setminus S_0$. Then, clearly, $-\widehat{w}$ is an optimal solution to the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $(M^* = (S, \mathcal{I}^*), S - x, -w, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$. 

    Conversely, for each $x \in S \setminus S_0$, let $w_x$ be an optimal solution to the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $(M^* = (S, \mathcal{I}^*), S - x, -w, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$. Then for any $x\in\argmin\{\|w-w_x\|_\infty\mid x\in S\setminus S_0\}$, the function $\widehat{w} = -w_x$ is an optimal solution to the original \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance.

    Therefore, the first part of the lemma follows by Lemma~\ref{lem:IM-Exists_special_weight}, while the second part, concerning half-integrality and the pair of elements $e$ and $f$, follows by Algorithm~\ref{alg:binary}.
\end{proof}

    The proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:relaxedProb_Nicer_special_weight_2}  gives a polynomial-time algorithm for \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists}: compute an optimal solution $w_x$, for each $x \in S \setminus S_0$, to the \textsc{IM-Exists} instance $(M^* = (S, \mathcal{I}^*), S - x, -w, \| \cdot \|_\infty)$, and then set $\widehat{w} = -w_x$ for any $x\in\argmin\{\|w-w_x\|_\infty\mid x\in S\setminus S_0\}$. However, we give a faster algorithm, presented as Algorithm~\ref{algo:relaxed-NOT-IM-Exists}. 


\begin{algorithm}[th!]
\caption{Binary search algorithm for \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} \label{algo:relaxed-NOT-IM-Exists}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ of \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists}.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight function $\widehat{w}$.}
    Let $L$ be the list of values $(w(e) - w(f))/2$ for all $e\in S_0$ and $f\in S \setminus S_0$ in an increasing order.\;
    Set $\ell \coloneqq 1$, $u\coloneqq|L|$.\;
    \While{$\ell \neq u $}{
        Set $\widehat{\delta}$ as the $\lfloor (\ell+u)/2\rfloor$-th element of the list $L$.\;
        Set $\widehat{w}(s) \coloneqq w(s) -\widehat{\delta}$ if $s \in S_0$ and $\widehat{w}(s)\coloneqq w(s) +\widehat{\delta}$ if $s \in S \setminus S_0$.\;  
        Let $B$ be a basis obtained by running the greedy algorithm with respect to $\widehat{w}$ while breaking ties in favour of elements in $S\setminus S_0$.\;
        \If{$B\not\subseteq S_0$}{
            Update $u\gets \lfloor(\ell+u)/2\rfloor$.\;
            }
        \Else{ 
            Update $\ell\gets \lceil(\ell+u)/2\rceil$. \;
            }   
        }
    \Return{$\widehat{w}$}\;
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:algo-relaxedProb}
    Algorithm~\ref{algo:relaxed-NOT-IM-Exists} determines an optimal weight function $\widehat{w}$ for the \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ with $w\in\bR^S$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    The correctness of the algorithm follows by Lemma~\ref{lem:relaxedProb_Nicer_special_weight_2}. Each step of the algorithm can be performed in polynomial time, thus the theorem follows.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:relaxedProb_Nicer_special_weight_2} has another interesting consequence. Namely, if integrality constraints are added on the weight function $\widehat{w}$ in \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists}, then an optimal solution can be obtained from an optimal fractional solution in a straightforward manner.

\begin{cor}\label{cor:rine}
    Let $\widehat{\delta}$ be the optimum value of a \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \widehat{w}_\bZ(s) = \begin{cases}
            w(s) - \big\lceil\,\widehat{\delta}\,\big\rceil &  \text{if $s \in S_0$} , \\
            w(s) + \big\lceil\, \widehat{\delta}\,\big\rceil & \text{if $s \in S \setminus S_0$}
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    is an optimal solution to the same \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance under integrality constraints.
\end{cor}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm-not-IM-Exists}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We now return to our original problem, \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}. Our algorithm relies on the following observation, which connects the relaxed and original versions.

   \begin{lemma}\label{lem:isudufb}   
        Let $\widehat{\delta}\in\bZ_+$ be the optimum value for a \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \allowbreak\|\cdot\|_\infty )$ under integrality constraints. Then either $w+\widehat{\delta}\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$ or $w+(\widehat{\delta}+1)\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$ is an optimal solution to the \textsc{IM-Not-Exists} problem on the same instance.
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}         
        For ease of discussion, let $w'\coloneqq w+\widehat{\delta}\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:rine}, $w'$ is an optimal solution for \textsc{Relaxed-IM-No-Exists}. If this weight function is feasible for \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}, then it is optimal as well as the former problem is a relaxation of the latter. Otherwise, there exists a basis $B_0 \subseteq S_0$ which has maximum $w'$-weight. Note that, by definition of $\widehat{\delta}$, there exists a basis $B\not \subseteq S_0$ that has maximum $w'$-weight. Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:Not-IM-Exists_easy_case} for the instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w', \|\cdot\|_\infty )$, the statement follows.
    \end{proof}

Our algorithm is presented as Algorithm~\ref{algo:NOT-IM-Exists}.

\begin{algorithm}[h!]
\caption{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}~\label{algo:NOT-IM-Exists}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ of \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight for \textsc{IM-Not-Exists}.}
    % \If {$w$ is feasible} {
    %     Set $w^*\coloneqq w$.\;
    % }
    % \Else{
        \vspace{2pt}
        Let $\widehat{\delta}$ be the optimal value of \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} for instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ with integrality constraints.\;
        \If{$w+\widehat{\delta}\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$ is feasible}
            {
               Set $w^*\coloneqq w+\widehat{\delta}\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$.\;
            }
        \Else{
            Set $w^*\coloneqq w+(\widehat{\delta}+1)\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus S_0}-\chi_{S_0})$.\;
        }
    % }   
    \Return{$w^*$}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:NOT-IM-Exists-algorithm}
    Algorithm~\ref{algo:NOT-IM-Exists} determines an optimal weight function $w^*$ for the \textsc{IM-Not-Exists} instance  $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    The correctness of the algorithm follows by Lemma~\ref{lem:isudufb}. As one can find a solution of \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} with integrality constraints in polynomial time by Corollary~\ref{cor:rine}, the theorem follows.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{\textsc{Inverse Matroid Not All} and \textsc{Not Only}}\label{sec:not-IM-All}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In the negated version of \textsc{IM-All}, the goal is to modify the weight function so that at least one basis contained in $S_0$ is not of maximum weight. As before, to avoid dealing with $\varepsilon$-perturbations, we consider integer-valued weight functions throughout.

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid Not All (IM-Not-All)}
    {A matroid $M = (S, \mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that contains a basis and not another, a weight function $w \in \mathbb{Z}^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\mathbb{R}^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bZ^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item{\label{it:feas_cond_not-IM-All}} there exists a basis contained in $S_0$ that is not of maximum $w^*$-weight, and
        \item \label{it:opt_cond_not-IM-All} $\|w - w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}} 

We say that a weight function is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-All} holds. Note that \textsc{IM-Not-All} is always feasible: set $w'$ such that $w'(s)=0$ for all $s \in S_0$ and $w'(s)=1$ for all $s \in S \setminus S_0$.

\begin{remark}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-Not-All}. To do so, find a minimum $w'$-weight basis $B_0$ of $M|S_0$ and a maximum $w'$-weight basis $A$ of $M$. Thus, $w'$ is feasible if and only if $w'(B_0) < w'(A)$.
\end{remark}

We also consider the negated version of \textsc{IM-Only}, which is formally defined as follows.

\problemdef{Inverse Matroid Not Only (IM-Not-Only)}
    {A matroid $M=(S,\mathcal{I})$, a subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ that contains a basis or does not contain some basis, a weight function $w\in\bR^S$, and an objective function $\|\cdot\|$ defined on $\bR^S$.}
    {Find a weight function $w^* \in \bZ^S$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),topsep=1px,partopsep=2px]\itemsep0em
        \item{\label{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only}} either
            \begin{enumerate}[label=(a.\arabic*), topsep=1px,partopsep=2px,itemsep=0pt]
                \item{\label{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-1}} there exists a basis contained in $S_0$ that is not of maximum $w^*$-weight, or
                \item{\label{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-2}}  there exists a basis not in $S_0$ that has maximum $w^*$-weight, 
            \end{enumerate}
        \item[] and        
        \item \label{it:opt_cond_not-IM-Only} $\| w - w^*\|$ is minimized.
    \end{enumerate}}

We say that a weight function is \emph{feasible} if condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only} holds. Note that a feasible weight function always exists: set $w'$ such that $w'(s)=0$ for all $s \in S_0$ and $w'(s)=1$ for all $s \in S \setminus S_0$. 

\begin{remark}\label{rem:IM-NOT-ONLY_feasible}
    Given a weight function $w'$, we can verify in polynomial time whether $w'$ is feasible for \textsc{IM-Not-Only}. This follows by noticing that that condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-1} is exactly condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-All} of \textsc{IM-Not-All}, and condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-2} is condition~\ref{it:feas_cond_relaxed} of \textsc{Relaxed-IM-Not-Exists} when the input set is $S_0$. 
\end{remark} 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Preparations}
\label{sec:prepnot}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Similarly to the case of \textsc{IM-All} and \textsc{IM-Only}, we will use the fact that \textsc{IM-Not-All} and \textsc{IM-Not-Only} admit an optimal solution with a very restricted structure.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:not}
Let $w_\textsc{Feas}$ be a feasible solution for the \textsc{IM-Not-All} or \textsc{IM-Not-Only} instance $(M=(S,\cI),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$, and let $\delta=\|w-w_\textsc{Feas}\|_\infty$. Then there exists a feasible solution $w'_\textsc{Feas}$ satisfying the following:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]\itemsep0em
    \item $\|w-w'_\textsc{Feas}\|_\infty=\delta$, and \label{prop:aa}
    \item $w'_\textsc{Feas}=w + \delta\cdot (\chi_{S\setminus B_0}-\chi_{B_0})$ for some $B_0\subseteq S_0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $B_0\subseteq S_0$ be a basis that is not of maximum $w_\textsc{Feas}$-weight and let $B_{\max}$ be a basis of maximum $w_\textsc{Feas}$-weight -- such bases exist since $w_\textsc{Feas}$ is feasible. For $w'_\textsc{Feas}=w+\delta\cdot (\chi_{S\setminus B_0}-\chi_{B_0})$, we get 
    \begin{align*}
        w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0)
        &=
        w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\setminus B_{\max})+w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\cap B_{\max})\\
        &\leq 
        w_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\setminus B_{\max})+w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\cap B_{\max})\\
        &< 
        w_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_{\max}\setminus B_0)+w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\cap B_{\max})\\
        &\leq 
        w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_{\max}\setminus B_0)+w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0\cap B_{\max})\\
        &=
        w'_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_{\max}),
    \end{align*}
    where the strict inequality holds by $w_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_0)<w_{\textsc{Feas}}(B_{\max})$. That is, $w'_\textsc{Feas}$ is a feasible solution that satisfies the conditions of the lemma.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Not-All}}
\label{sec:algnotall}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Our algorithm is presented as Algorithm~\ref{algo:NOT-IM-All}.

\begin{algorithm}[th!]
\caption{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Not-All}~\label{algo:NOT-IM-All}}
\DontPrintSemicolon
\KwIn{An instance $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ of \textsc{IM-Not-All}.}
\KwOut{An optimal weight for \textsc{IM-Not-All}.}
    \If {$w$ is feasible} {
        Set $w^*\coloneqq w$.\;
    }
    \Else{
        Let $B_0\subseteq S_0$ be a basis of maximum $w$-weight.\;
        \If{$w-\chi_{B_0}$ is feasible} {
            Set $w^*\coloneqq w-\chi_{B_0}$.\;
        \Else{        
            Set $\delta\coloneqq \min\{\lceil(w(e)-w(f)+1)/2\rceil\mid f\in S\setminus B_0,e\in C(B_0,f)\}$.\;
            Set $w^*\coloneqq w+\delta\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus B_0}-\chi_{B_0})$.\;
        }
    }
    }
    \Return{$\widehat{w}$}\;
\end{algorithm}

\begin{thm}\label{thm:NOT-IM-All-algorithm}
    Algorithm~\ref{algo:NOT-IM-All} determines an optimal weight function $w^*$ for the \textsc{IM-Not-All} instance  $( M=(S, \cI),  S_0, w, \|\cdot\|_\infty )$ in polynomial time.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
    If $S_0$ contains a basis that is not of maximum $w$-weight, then $w$ is feasible and we are done. Otherwise, all bases in $S_0$ have maximum weight. Let $B_0\subseteq S_0$ be an arbitrary basis contained in $S_0$. 

    Assume first that there exists a basis $B_{\max}$ of maximum $w$-weight different from $B_0$. Note that $B_{\max}$ is allowed to be contained in $S_0$. We claim that $w'=w-\chi_{B_0}$ is a feasbile solution. Indeed, $w'(B_0)=w(B_0)-|B_0|\leq w(B_{\max})-|B_{\max}\cap B_0|=w'(B_{\max})$, hence $w'$ is feasible. Since $w$ itself is not feasible, $w'$ is optimal as well, due to the integrality constraint.

    Suppose now that $B_0$ is the unique maximum $w$-weight basis of the matroid. If $\delta$ denotes the optimum value of the problem then, by Lemma~\ref{lem:not}, $w+\delta\cdot(\chi_{S\setminus B_0}-\chi_{B_0})$ is an optimal solution. This also implies that the optimum value is
    \[\delta=\min\Bigl\{\Bigl\lceil\frac{w(B_0)-w(B)+1}{|B_0\triangle B|}\Bigr\rceil\Bigm| B\in\cB\Bigr\},\]
    where the $+1$ is needed to make the weight of $B$ strictly greater than that of $B_0$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:IM-offline-infty_symmDiff_equals_2}\ref{it:b}, the minimum is attained on a basis $B$ with $|B_0\triangle B|=2$, leading to the algorithm provided.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Algorithm for \textsc{IM-Not-Only}}
\label{sec:algnotonly}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Finally, by Remark~\ref{rem:IM-NOT-ONLY_feasible} we show that \textsc{IM-Not-Only} can be solved using our previous algorithms. To see this, observe that one can find a weight function $w_1$ that satisfies~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-1}  and minimizes $\|w-w_1\|_\infty$ using Algorithm~\ref{algo:NOT-IM-All}, and also a weight function $w_2$ that satisfies~\ref{it:feas_cond_not-IM-Only-2} and minimizes $\|w-w_2\|_\infty$ using Algorithm~\ref{algo:relaxed-NOT-IM-Exists}. Then, the best of these two solutions, i.e. the one that has smaller distance from $w$ in $\ell_\infty$-norm, is an optimal solution for~\textsc{IM-Not-Only}.

\begin{cor}\label{cor:NOT-IM-All-solution}
    Given an \textsc{IM-Not-Only} instance $(M=(S,\cI),S_0,w,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$, an optimal weight function can be computed in polynomial time.
\end{cor}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this paper, we presented a thorough analysis of various extensions of the inverse matroid problem under subset constraints. Given a fixed set $S_0$, we provided algorithms for problems in which the goal is to modify the weight function so that the matroid has at least one, all, or exactly the bases contained in $S_0$ as maximum-weight bases. We also examined the negated versions of these problems, resulting in efficient algorithms for six naturally arising variants.

It is worth noting that our results extend to the more general setting where lower and upper bounds are given on the coordinates of the modified weight function. However, the corresponding algorithms become more involved due to numerous case distinctions, which would detract from the main structural insights that are more easily presented in the unconstrained setting. Moreover, some of the problems remain easy to solve under the weighted $\ell_\infty$-norm $\| p \|_{\omega} = \max \{ |p(s)|\cdot \omega(s) \mid s \in S \}$, where $\omega\in\bR_+^S$ represents the ``cost'' of modifying each coordinate of $w$.

We conclude the paper by mentioning some open questions:
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep0em
    \item Throughout the paper, we used the $\ell_\infty$-norm to measure the distance between the original and modified weight functions. It would be interesting to investigate the complexity landscape of the considered problems under alternative ``distance measures''.
    \item We introduced six variants of the \textsc{Inverse Matroid} problem, all of which can also be formulated for the intersection of two matroids. Designing efficient algorithms for this setting would be particularly interesting due to its applications to bipartite matchings and arborescences.
    \item Subset-constrained variants of \textsc{Inverse Optimization} can be considered in general, although certain settings may lead to NP-hard problems. An intriguing direction is to identify structural assumptions on $\mathcal{F}$ or the underlying optimization problem under which the proposed variants remain tractable.
\end{enumerate}

