\section*{Preliminary test at the LHC}

The Large Hadron Collider (LHC) offers a complementary platform to probe the temporal dynamics of quantum state formation through decay channels of the spin-singlet toponium states \(\eta_t(nS)\)~\cite{Fu:2025zxb}. In particular, we consider the branching ratio
\begin{equation}
R_{ZH}^A = \frac{\sigma(pp \to \sum_n \eta_t(nS) \to ZH)}{\sigma(pp \to \sum_n \eta_t(nS) \to t\bar{t})},
\end{equation}
which is sensitive to the parameter \(A\) encoding the temporal formation mechanism.

Assuming degenerate masses and widths across the $nS$ spectrum, the signal rate for \(\eta_t(nS) \to ZH\) scales as \(e^{-4A n^3 t_1 / \tau_t} / n^6\), yielding distinct predictions under the two hypotheses:
\begin{align}
R_{ZH}^{A=0} &= (18.3 \pm 1.8) \times 10^{-4}, \\
R_{ZH}^{A=1} &= (8.3 \pm 0.8) \times 10^{-4}.
\end{align}

With approximately 600\,fb$^{-1}$ of data already collected at the LHC, this branching ratio difference corresponds to a potential \(2\)–\(3\sigma\) level sensitivity~\cite{Yuan:2025,CMS:2025kzt}, offering preliminary evidence in favor of a causal formation delay.

Although not definitive, this result provides independent support for the collider-based framework proposed here. It also highlights the potential for further exploration at the High-Luminosity LHC, where increased statistics and improved systematics could significantly enhance sensitivity to the quantum temporal structure of bound state formation.


\section*{Discussion and outlook}

We have introduced a collider-accessible framework to directly probe the fundamental tension between quantum superposition and relativistic causality at the yoctosecond scale (\(10^{-24}\,\mathrm{s}\)). By exploiting the top quark’s ultrashort lifetime as an intrinsic quantum clock, we investigate whether quantum states emerge instantaneously, as dictated by the superposition principle, or evolve over a finite causal time, as required by relativistic locality.

This temporal dichotomy manifests in observable suppression patterns in toponium formation amplitudes. In particular, the cross-section ratio \(R_b\) near threshold at future lepton colliders is shown to provide a clean and statistically significant discriminator. Our simulations indicate that CEPC or FCC-ee, with 1500\,fb$^{-1}$ of data, can distinguish between instantaneous and causal formation scenarios at greater than \(5\sigma\) significance. Preliminary LHC measurements of \(\eta_t(nS)\to ZH\) and \(\bar{t}t\) decay modes already provide supporting evidence at the \(2\)–\(3\sigma\) level.

These results suggest a possible breakdown or refinement of wave-particle duality in ultrafast, high-energy regimes. Unlike traditional Bell tests, which probe spatial nonlocality, our approach is inherently temporal, providing a direct probe of how coherence and wavefunction collapse unfold in time. This opens a new experimental avenue in the foundations of quantum theory, with far-reaching implications for relativistic quantum information, quantum field theory, and the operational limits of causality.

Future improvements—including increased luminosity, energy scan optimization, and better control over systematic uncertainties—will sharpen the sensitivity of this framework. As high-energy colliders advance toward precision quantum tests, they may soon offer definitive answers to one of the most fundamental questions at the intersection of quantum mechanics and relativity.


