\subsection{Neko and Small Matrix Multiply Kernel} CFD codes are one of the workhorses of HPC. Among the different codes, Neko, a new updated Nek5000 descendant, has exhibited excellent parallel performance for large-scale runs, including full-scale LUMI \cite{10.1145/3581784.3627039}. The code has been validated with several tests, and applied to different grand challnges, including the Flettner rotor and Rayleigh-Bénard convection case.

Differently from it ancestor, Neko code is written in modern Fortran, employing dynamic memory management, and object oriented structured exploiting one unified interface for implementations for different back ends.

Neko is based on the spectral element method. In this method, the simulation is divided into several elements that spectrally discretized.

The two main ingredients of the Neko code are the matrix-multiply and the gather-scatter operation. The matrix-multiply is typically of small size as the size depends on the order of the spectral element.

\section{The spectral element method / Matrix-free high-order method}
\textcolor{blue}{This text might be shorter and included in background}. This text investigates performance portability of a single test-kernel that arises 
in the CFD code Neko. We will investigate a reduced problem which is only a part of the full Navier-Stokes solver that Neko uses, namely the part that generates the matrix-free "assembly" of the Poisson equation which is a major component. Here is the standard weak form formulation of Poissons equation, which is the starting point for any FEM solver. 
\begin{align}
    \int_{\Omega} \nabla u \nabla v \operatorname{d\Omega} = \int_{\Omega} f v \operatorname{d\Omega}, \forall v \in V,
\end{align}
where $\Omega$ is the domain and $v$ any test function in $V$. 

The spectral element method is a FEM method based on the a specific choice of elements which uses N\textsuperscript{th}-order Legendre polynomials $l_i$ interpolated on the Gauss-Lobatto-Legendre (GLL) quadrature points $\xi$ that lives on the refrence element. 

\begin{align}
    l_i(\xi) = \frac{N(1-\xi^2 L_N(\xi) }{(N+1)(\xi-\xi_i)L_N(\xi_i)}, \quad \xi \in [-1,1]. 
\end{align}

The spectral element solution $u_h$ that lives in $V_h$ can the be expressed as a tensor product on the reference element/(GLOBAL?) ($u_h^e$), 

\begin{align}
    u_h^e(\xi,\eta,\gamma) = \sum_{i,j,k}^N u_{ijk}l_i(\xi)l_j(\eta)l_k(\gamma)
\end{align}


