\section{Additional proofs}

\subsection{Properties of the $\maxdeg$ function}\label{app:maxdeg}

In Remark \ref{r:products of AD-maximal}, we mentioned that the product of two AD-maximal groups $G_1$ and $G_2$ is itself AD-maximal. We now give a brief explanation. First note that $G_1\times G_2$ is virtually abelian (since both $G_1$ and $G_2$ are). Then, since AD is multiplicative (Proposition \ref{p:AD properties}\ref{li:AD of product}),
it suffices to show that $\maxdeg(G_1\times G_2)=\maxdeg(G_1)\maxdeg(G_2)$.
This, in turn, will follow once we know that the natural map from $\widehat{G_1}\times\widehat{G_2}\to(G_1\times G_2)^\wedge$ given by by tensoring representations is bijective. The quickest way to justify this (although it is overkill) is to recall that since $G_1$ is virtually abelian it is Type~I (see Remark \ref{r:LCVA implies Type I}), and then appeal to \cite[Theorem 7.17]{Folland-book}.

Later in the paper, in Section \ref{s:prove upper bound}, we used the fact that for a discrete group $G$ and a subgroup $H\leq G$ we have $\maxdeg(H)\leq\maxdeg(G)$. For the reader's convenience, we provide a proof of this fact; the argument is adapted from the proof of \cite[Proposition 3.1]{Moore-fd-irrep}.
\begin{proof}[The proof of Lemma \ref{l:monotone}\ref{li:monotone maxdeg}]
We use the following result of Amitsur and Levitzki~\cite{Amitsur-Levitzki}: for each $n\in\Nat$ there exists a noncommutative polynomial $P_{2n}$ in $2n$ variables, with the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $1\leq d\leq n$, then $P_{2n}(X_1,\dots, X_{2n})=0$ for all $X_j\in M_d(\Cplx)$;
\item if $d\geq n+1$, then there exist $Y_1,\dots, Y_{2n}\in M_d(\Cplx)$ such that $P_{2n}(Y_1,\dots, Y_{2n})\neq 0$.
\end{itemize}

Let $m=\maxdeg(G)$, and consider the complex group ring $\Cplx G$. For each $\pi\in\widehat{G}$, the ring $\pi(\Cplx G)$ is contained in $\Bdd(H_\pi)$ where $d_\pi \leq m$, and so it satisfies the identity $P_{2m}=0$. Since $\widehat{G}$ separates the points of $\Cplx G$ (by e.g.\ the Gelfand--Raikov theorem), we deduce that $\Cplx G$ also satisfies this identity.

Since $\Cplx H$ is a unital subring of $\Cplx G$, it follows that $\Cplx H$ also satisfies $P_{2m}$. However, for each $\sigma\in\widehat{L}$, it follows from Schur's lemma and the bicommutant theorem that $\sigma(\Cplx L)$ is SOT-dense in $\Bdd(H_\sigma)$. Since multiplication in $\Bdd(H_\sigma)$ is separately SOT-continuous, by taking iterated limits it follows that $\Bdd(H_\sigma)$ also satisfies the identity $P_{2m}=0$. This forces $d_\sigma \leq m$, as required.
\end{proof}


%\newcommand{\Ghat}{\widehat{G}} % just to save time while typing
