\section{Conclusion}
\label{remarks}

The highly versatile nature of charged polymers or polyelectrolytes (PEs) provides enormous opportunities for controlled manipulation of nanoparticle's (NP) physicochemical properties. The adsorption of PE on the surfaces of NPs plays a critical role in modulating their stability and aggregation behavior in solution. This is crucial for various applications. In this study, we have systematically explored the influence of charge regulation (CR) on the interactions between two NPs coated with base groups in the presence of an oppositely charged single weak polyanion in a monovalent solution. By implementing a hybrid CR Monte Carlo/molecular dynamics (CR-MC/MD) simulation framework, we provide a mechanistic understanding of how dynamically regulated surface charges, in contrast to static constant charge (CC) approximations, impact PE adsorption behavior and modulate interparticle forces. 

At low salt concentrations ($pIp = pIm = 7$), our results show that CR significantly enhances PE adsorption on NP surfaces. This is also confirmed by the $Rg$ behavior. Importantly, the time evolution of the adsorbed monomer fraction is much faster and higher(2-fold) than that observed for CC. This adsorption strongly suppresses electrostatic repulsion by effectively neutralizing the NP surface charges, resulting in weak net interparticle interactions across a wide range of NPs separations. In the addition of salt, long-range, however weak, attractive interactions between like-charged NPs are obtained. The CC simulations demonstrate clear long-range attractions driven by polymer bridging, with the PE chains adopting extended conformations connecting both NPs. However, the CR scenario promotes localized adsorption, where the PE preferentially collapses onto a single NP, thereby eliminating the entropic and elastic contribution associated with bridging and reducing the net attractive interaction.

The dependence of intermolecular forces on PE chain length was found to be markedly different between these two models. While longer PE chains in the CC simulations enhance bridging and, consequently, attractive interactions, the CR simulations exhibit largely chain-length-independent behavior due to the saturation of available adsorption sites. This highlights the dominant role of surface ionization equilibrium in dictating the interaction landscape in the CR paradigm. We further investigated the role of acid-base dissociation constants by tuning the net ionization parameter, $\Delta pK = pK_a + pK_b - 2pH$. This parameter captures the joint contribution of the dissociable groups on both the PE and the NP surfaces. The results demonstrate that the difference between CR and CC is most significant at intermediate $\Delta pK$ values, where partial ionization allows CR to dynamically respond to local electrostatic environments, leading to stronger adsorption and reduced interparticle forces. Notably, CR consistently exhibits faster and more complete adsorption dynamics, emphasizing its responsiveness and kinetic favorability over the CC approximation. At higher salt concentrations ($pIp = pIm = 3$), electrostatic screening reduces the effective interactions in both CR and CC simulations. Under these conditions, the differences in the extent of adsorption and radius of gyration between the two models diminish. However, even in the high-salt regime, charge regulation continues to exhibit greater PE adsorption, although the difference in inter-particle forces becomes less pronounced. This underlines the ion-sensitive nature of charge regulation, where the balance between surface ionization and ionic screening governs both adsorption and colloidal/NPs stability. Our findings suggest that surface-tunable charge regulation can be a strategic tool for stabilizing nanoparticle suspensions under physiological ionic conditions. Future work could explore active tuning of CR by external stimuli such as electric fields or pH gradients.



