    \label{fig:bennett_pressure}
\end{figure}
\section*{Results}
\subsection*{Bennett Vortex}
When the plasma flow carries the nonlinearity, instead of the plasma density, the key difference is that the plasma pressure gradient is now supported by a temperature gradient, instead of a density gradient. This fact is extremely relevant in a Bennett pinch as the Bennett parameter, $b$, given by Equation (\ref{eqn:bennett_parameter_ideal}), now is host to a spatially-dependent temperature which must be dealt with, and introduces significant complexity into the governing equations which must be considered.
\\
\indent
The most important consideration is the ideal shear-flow stabilized Z-Pinch criterion,
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:shear_criterion_ideal}
    \dv{u_{z}}{r} > 0
\end{equation}
which can be derived from the general criterion, Equation (\ref{eqn:sfs_criterion}), 
\begin{align}
    \dv{u_{z}}{r} &> 0.1kV_{A} \nonumber \\
    &= 0.1(\frac{2\pi}{L}\frac{B}{\sqrt{\rho \mu_{0}}}) \nonumber \\
    &= \sqrt{\frac{\mu_{0}}{\rho}}\frac{\pi B\sigma u}{5R_{m}} \nonumber 
\end{align}
as $R_{m}\rightarrow\infty$, or equivalently here $L\rightarrow\infty$, then the above reduces to Equation (\ref{eqn:shear_criterion_ideal}). Fusion plasmas are predicted to behave as they do because of the high temperatures which imply an infinite conductivity\cite{Freidberg_2007}, but in practice alpha heating also means that high-fidelity modelling of fusion plasma dynamics requires the incorporation of relativistic physics. As it stands, the idea of Bennett vortices are most likeliest to be applicable to very long, coherent, astrophysical structures in regions of steady density.  
\\
\indent From a theoretical perspective it is perfectly valid to do this, but computationally the closest that can be achieved is the implementation of periodic axial boundary conditions. These are of course an imperfect representation of an infinite plasma as information from a specific point on the mesh will eventually begin interfering with itself as it flows through the periodic boundary. However, the goal of this short article is just to present the results of theoretically exploring the Bennett vortex as a shear-flow stabilized Z-pinch in the ideal limit. 
\\
\indent 
In this vein, the shear is described by,
\begin{align}
    \dv{u_{z}}{r} &= \dv{}{r}\left(\frac{u_{z,0}}{(1 + \xi^{2}r^{2})^{2}}\right) \nonumber \\
    &= \frac{-2u_{z,0}}{(1 + \xi^{2}r^{2})^{3}}\left[-r^{2}n_{0}\frac{b}{T}\dv{T}{r} + 2\xi^{2}r\right] \nonumber \\
    &= \frac{2\xi^{2}ru_{z,0}}{(1 + \xi^{2}r^{2})^{3}}\left[\frac{r}{T}\dv{T}{r} - 2\right] \label{eqn:bennett_vortex_shear}
\end{align}
and can be considered as being composed of two terms,
\begin{align}
    I &= \frac{2\xi^{2}ru_{z,0}}{(1 + \xi^{2}r^{2})^{3}} \\
    II &= \frac{r}{T}\dv{T}{r} - 2
\end{align}
Conformance to the ideal form of the SFS Z-Pinch criterion requires that neither of I and II be zero, and that both either be positive, or negative. It is simplest to first consider the case when $II > 0$ as this gives the requirement that,
\begin{align}
    \frac{r}{T}\dv{T}{r} &> 2 \\
    \rightarrow \frac{dT}{T} &> \frac{2}{r}dr \\
    \therefore \ln{T} &> 2\ln{r} \\
    \therefore T(r) &> r^{2}
\end{align}
The simplest thing to do is plug $T(r) \sim r^{2}$ into I, and then check for positivity, as this will yield the correct expression for I up to a positive constant, $C_{T} > 1$, i.e., $T(r) = C_{T}r^{2}$, that will not influence any positive-definite structure. This yields
\begin{align}
    \frac{2\xi^{2}ru_{z,0}}{(1 + \xi^{2}r^{2})^{3}} &= \frac{2bn_{0}ru_{z,0}}{(1 + bn_{0}r^{2})^{3}} \\
    &=2u_{z,0}C_{B}\frac{r}{T}\frac{1}{(1 + \frac{C_{B}}{T}n_{0}r^{2})^{3}} \\
    &\sim \frac{2u_{z,0}C_{B}}{r}\frac{1}{(1 + C_{B}n_{0})^{3}} \\
    &> 0
\end{align}
which confirms that Bennett vortices with temperature profiles where $T(r) > r^{2}$ everywhere conform to the SFS criterion in the ideal limit. 
\\
\indent 
One thing to note about the above is that this portion of the shear blows up at the origin, and that it is positive everywhere except there and at infinity. However, the plasma state is taken to be trivial in the latter case, so that fact is inconsequential. Lastly, in the above the Bennett parameter is expressed as, 
\begin{equation}
    b = \frac{C_{B}}{T}
\end{equation}
where,
\begin{equation}
    C_{B} = \frac{\mu_{0}e^{2}u_{z,0}^{2}}{16k_{B}}
\end{equation}
Before continuing on to the negative case, we can address the singularity problem by incorporating a fixed core plasma temperature so that it does not have a trivial solution at the origin. Meaning,
\begin{equation}
    T(r) = T_{0} + C_{T}r^{2}
\end{equation}
A consideration of $II$ under the above implies a condition governing the specified core temperature,
\begin{equation}
    T_{0} > r^{2}(1 - C_{T})
\end{equation}
with $C_{T} > 1$ this is everywhere satisfied. Turning to $I$, we find
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn:sfs_Iplus_coretempincl}
    2C_{B}u_{z,0}\frac{r(T_{0}+C_{T}r^{2})^{2}}{\left[T_{0} + r^{2}(C_{T} + C_{B}n_{0})\right]^{3}} > 0
\end{equation}
The denominator of Equation (\ref{eqn:sfs_Iplus_coretempincl}) is an everywhere-positive collection of terms which is raised to the third power so itself is everywhere-positive. Dividing out these components leaves the condition,
\begin{equation}
    r(T_{0} + C_{T}r^{2})^{2} > 0
\end{equation}
Which is satisfied everywhere except at the origin because of the positive-definite nature of the radius. 
% \subsection*{$I < 0$ \\ $II < 0$}
\\
\indent 
If II were instead considered to be negative, then obviously the resulting condition on the temperature gradient is, 
\begin{equation}
    T < r^{2}    
\end{equation}
A similar procedure can be performed as in the positive case, however, it should be noted that there is a much larger space now for the possible temperature profiles to occupy and that this condition must be everywhere satisfied in the plasma volume. Furthermore, note that \textit{prima facie} this space could also include negative temperatures which are irrelevant to fusion plasma reactor science. Leaving these aside, finding a temperature profile that conforms to the above is more challenging than in the positive case because any higher-order-than-second profile will fail conformance when the radius grows long enough,
\begin{align}
    r^{n} &< r^{2} \\
    \rightarrow r^{n-2} &< 1
\end{align}
It is also not tractable to reuse the form $T(r) = T_{0} + C_{T}r^{2}$ with a different value for the parabolic coefficient because the existence of a positive core temperature will render this incoherent. In fact, any positive core temperature will carry the same consequence so this line is not analyzed further.    
\section*{Future Work and Conclusion}
The Bennett vortex expresses a valid SFS Z-pinch equilibrium in the ideal limit, $R_{m}\rightarrow\infty$ when the plasma temperature is everywhere larger than a parabola. Of course, the Bennett vortex is just a toy model for a SFS Z-Pinch that is based on Ideal MHD, meaning certain physics is neglected which is important in real SFS Z-Pinches. The focus of contemporary researchers in this direction concerns themselves with a revisitation of the marginally-stable Kadomtsev pinch which features a pressure profile tailored to stabilize the $m = 0$ sausage mode,
\begin{equation}
-\dv{\ln p}{\ln r} \leq \frac{4 \Gamma}{2 + \Gamma\beta}    
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma$ is the ratio of specific heats, and $\beta = \frac{2\mu_{0}p}{B^{2}}$. However, accurate modeling of real-world fusion physics requires the usage of relativistic physics to capture the behavior of supra-thermal electrons which are engendered by alpha heating. Next steps in this direction would be well-chosen if they instead utilized a relativistic electron fluid, e.g., a moment-taking process based on a Maxwell-Juttner distribution. Furthermore, at a minimum this relativistic electron fluid needs to be taken as part of a multi-fluid model which also treats thermal electrons, and the alphas.  
\\
\indent 
Theoretical investigation is just one of the three cornerstones of scientific inquiry. To accompany the work presented here, and further explore the nature of the Bennett vortex as expressing a state of shear-flow stabilization, numerical experiments can naturally be carried out in this direction. Additionally, modern computational fusion science benefits from a natural coupling to GPU computing as compact fusion devices offer the promise of powering massive datacenters while GPUs provide the high-performance targets for the high-performance scientific applications that modern fusion plasma pinch science needs, like \href{https://github.com/russellmatt66/imhd-CUDA}{imhd-CUDA} an open-source, CUDA C, 3D, time-dependent, FVM-based solver which the author has written. 
\\
\indent 
imhd-CUDA solves the equations of Ideal MHD on an orthogonal mesh, using an FVM, and is an HPC application which is appropriate for this kind of work as it takes only half an hour of walltime to fully render video of flowing MHD equilibria being solved on a 64M element mesh for 100 timesteps. Furthermore, imhd-CUDA possesses a modular structure which allows the usage of tokens to hash into the launching of arbitrary CUDA kernels via C++. Additionally, imhd-CUDA offers users a simple Python frontend launcher that abstracts away the encapsulating C++, and low-level, high-performing, CUDA C kernels.
