in particular  guarantee that  $\hat J>0$ for all $Z\ge 0$ and $0\le\xi\le l$, so that no 
wave-breaking during the laser-plasma interaction (WBDLPI) takes place. \
Sufficient conditions on the input data for ES %(resp. SS) 
pulses are given in \cite{FioDeAFedGueJov22,FioDeNAkhFedJov23}.
With stronger conditions (SC) 
on the input data one could ensure that $\big|\hat J-1\big|\ll 1$ for $0\le\xi\le \xi_1^0$.



\subsection{Special case: \ constant initial density}
\label{specialcase}

If \ $\widetilde{n_0}( Z ) =\bar{n}$ \ then 
%the longitudinal electric force  acting at time $t$ on the electrons initially located at $\bX$  is
% the force  of a harmonic oscillator (with equilibrium at $z_e\!=\!Z$) 
%$\widetilde{F_e^z}\!(t,\!Z)\!=\!-e \widetilde{E^z}\!(t,\!Z) \!=\!- 4\pi \bar{n} e^2 \Delta (t\!,\! Z)$; \ 
the $Z$-dependence disappears completely from
(\ref{heq1}-\ref{heq2}), which  reduces to the  Cauchy problem of a forced, relativistic harmonic oscillator with equilibrium at $\Delta\!=\!0$:
\bea
\hat\Delta'=\displaystyle\frac {1\!+\!v}{2\hat s^2}\!-\!\frac 12,\qquad\quad
\hat s'=M\hat\Delta,\qquad \qquad \hat\Delta(0)\!=\!0, \quad \hat s(0)\!=\! 1,
\label{e1}
\eea
 where $ M \!\equiv\!K\bar{n}\!=\!4\pi e^2\bar{n}/mc^2 \!=\!\omega_p^2/c^2%\!:=\!K\bar{n}
$ ($\omega_p$ is the {\it plasma angular frequency}).  The solution  of (\ref{e1}), as well as  $h$, $\hat\bu_e,\hat\BD$, depend on $\bar{n}$, but not on $Z$; it describes the motion  of every $Z$ layer of electrons. From $\partial\hat\Delta/\partial Z\!\equiv\!0$ it follows $\hat J\!=\!1$, which guarantees the HR for ever. In the sequel we denote by $\bar P(\xi;\bar{n})\equiv \big(\Delta(\xi 
;\bar{n}),s(\xi;\bar{n})\big)$ or more synthetically by $\bar P(\xi)\equiv \big(\Delta(\xi),s(\xi)\big)$
the solution of (\ref{e1}), by $\bar{h}(\bar{n}),\ospm,\osmm,\osM,\oDM,\oDm,\bar P_i,\bxiH,\ouxi^i,\bar\xi^i_k...$ the associated  $h,\spm,\smm,\sM,\DM,\Dm,P_i,\xiH,\uxi^i,\xi^i_k,...$. %As the potential energy in (\ref{hamiltonian}) takes the $Z$-independent form 
Since $\U\!\equiv\!M\Delta^2/2$, also $\oDM\!=\!-\oDm\!=\!\sqrt{2(\bar{h}\!-\!1)/M}$ is
$Z$-independent.
The inverse functions $Z_e(t,\!z)$ and $\hat Z_e(\xi,\!z)$ of $z_e(t,\!Z),\hat z_e(\xi,\!Z)$ have the closed form  
\be
 Z_e(t,z)=z-\Delta(ct\!-\!z)\qquad\Leftrightarrow\qquad \hat Z_e(\xi,z)=z\!-\!\Delta(\xi),                                                \label{sol"}
\ee 
