\subsection{WFA of injected electrons in the plane model}

%\medskip
If a test electron is injected  in the PW at $\xi_0\!>\!l$ with $\hbup_i(\xi_0)=0$,
then   its $\hat z_i,\hat s_i$ evolve after equations (\ref{reduced}), which become \cite{FioDeNAkhFedJov23} 
\bea
\hat z_i'= \displaystyle\frac {1\!-\!\hat s_i^2}{2\hat s_i^2}, \quad 
\hat s_i'(\xi)=K\!\left\{\!
\widetilde{N}\!\left[\hat z_i(\xi) \right] \!-\! \widetilde{N}\!\left[\hat Z_e\!\left(\xi,\!\hat z_i(\xi) \right) \right] \!\right\}\!.\qquad                 \label{heq-test}
\eea
Since $\widetilde{N}(z)=\widetilde{N}(z_s)\!+\!\bar{n} (z\!-\! z_s)$ for $z\ge z_s$,
by (\ref{sol"}) along the density plateau  (\ref{heq-test}b) reduces to $\hat s_i'=M\Delta$, 
i.e. the same equation (\ref{e1}b) fulfilled by $s(\xi)$. If we impose intial conditions 
$\big(\hat z_i,\hat s_i\big)_{\xi=\xi_0}\!=\!(z_{i0}, s_{i0})$, with $s_{i0}\!>\!0$,  $z_{i0}\!\ge\! z_q\!\equiv\! z_s\!+\!\DM(\bar{n})$, then
\be
\hat s_i(\xi)%=s_0+M\!\!\int^\xi_{\xi_0}\!\!\!\! dy\,\Delta(y)
=\delta s+s(\xi),\quad\hat z_i(\xi)=z_{i0}+\!\!\int^\xi_{\xi_0}\!\!  \frac{dy}2\left[\frac {1}{\hat s_i^2(y)}\!-\!1\right],  \label{test-motion}
\ee
as long as $\hat z_i\!\ge\! z_q\!\equiv\! z_s\!+\!\DM(\bar{n})$.
 Here  $s(\xi)$ is the $s(\xi;\bar{n})$ of section \ref{specialcase}, and \ $\delta s\equiv s_{i0}\!-\!s(\xi_0)$. \ If 
\be
s_i^m\equiv \osmm\!+\!\delta s < 0, \label{trap}
\ee
%the \ {\bf trapping condition} \cite{FioDeNAkhFedJov23}% \ $s_i^m\equiv \smm\!+\!\delta s < 0$ \ is fulfilled, 
then  $\hat s_i(\xi_f)=0$, $\hat s_i'(\xi_f)=s'(\xi_f)<0$ for some $\xi_f\!>\!\xi_0$ (corresponding to $t_f=\infty$):  the electron  remains trapped in the bucket of the PW located around $\xi=\xi_f$ while its velocity grows and goes to $c$. 
{\bf Inequality (\ref{trap})  is the trapping condition} \cite{FioDeNAkhFedJov23} that $\delta s$ must fulfill.
For $\xi\simeq \xi_f$ we have $\hat s_i(\xi)\simeq \! \left|s' (\xi_f)\right|(\xi_f\!-\!\xi)=\! M\left|\Delta (\xi_f)\right|(\xi_f\!-\!\xi)$, and in the limit $\xi\to \xi_f$
\be
\hat z_i(\xi)\, \simeq   \,
\frac {1}{2\left[ M\Delta(\xi_f)\right]^2 (\xi_f\!-\!\xi)}
\: \to %\stackrel{\xi\to \xi_f}{-\!\!\!-\!\!\!\longrightarrow} 
\: \infty. 
\label{approx}
\ee
Meanwhile,  $c\hat t(\xi)-\hat z_i(\xi)-\xi_f=\xi-\xi_f\stackrel{\xi\to \xi_f}{-\!\!\!-\!\!\!\longrightarrow}0$, implying $\big(t,z_i(t)\big)-\big(t,ct\!-\!\xi_f\big)\stackrel{t\to  \infty}{-\!\!\!-\!\!\!\longrightarrow}0$: as $t\to  \infty$ the test electron tends to the travelling point of  the bucket having spacetime coordinates $(t,ct\!-\!\xi_f)$. On the latter the longitudinal electric field has the constant value $\bar E^z=s'(\xi_f)mc^2/e=4\pi e\bar{n}\Delta(\xi_f)$; these equalities are based on (\ref{reduced}b), (\ref{e1}).
In this simplified model trapped test electrons {\it cannot dephase},
as the phase velocity of the PW is $c$.
Solving (\ref{approx}) for $\xi_f\!-\!\xi$ we can express first $\hat s_i$, then $\hat \gamma_i$ in terms of $ z_i$:
\be
\gamma_i=\frac{1}{2 s_i}+\frac{s_i}2
\:\simeq \: F \: \frac{z_i}{\lambda}\: \stackrel{ z_i\to \infty}{-\!\!\!-\!\!\!-\!\!\!\longrightarrow} \: \infty, \label{s_i^m<0|s_iz_i}
\ee
$F\!\equiv\! M\lambda \left|\Delta(\xi_f)\right|$.
Hence, in this simplified model  {\bf the  energy grows proportionally to the travelled distance}. 
Of course, (\ref{s_i^m<0|s_iz_i}) is reliable in the interval $0\le z_i\le \znd$ where the pulse depletion and deformation can be neglected.
Fixed  $z_i, \bar{n}$, the maximal rate $F$ of acceleration %of the injected electron 
is obtained maximizing $|\bar E^z|=4\pi e|\Delta(\xi_f)|$; this is achieved if
$\xi_f=\bar\xi_k^0$ for some $k\in\NN_0$, so that
 $|\Delta(\xi_f)|$ takes  its maximum value $|\Dm|\!=\!\sqrt{2\big(\bar{h}\!-\!1\big)/M}$ (see section \ref{specialcase}). Thus we obtain 
\be
\gamma_i(z_i; \bar{n}) \: \simeq \: \sqrt{ j(\nu)}\:\:  
\frac{z_i}{\lambda}, \qquad j(\nu)\equiv 8\pi^2\nu\big[\bar h(\nu)\!-\!1\big];         \label{gamma^M(z_i)}
\ee
here $\bar h(\nu)$ is the final electron energy transfered by the pulse if $\widetilde{n_0}(z)\!=\!\bar{n}$, as a function of $\nu\!\equiv\!\bar{n}/n_{cr}$. In this case $F(\nu)=\sqrt{ j(\nu)}= eE^z_{\scriptscriptstyle M}/mc^2$, where $E^z_{\scriptscriptstyle M}$   is  the maximal value reached by the longitudinal electric field (along the plateau of height $\bar{n}$) during its oscillations. By (\ref{test-motion}a), (\ref{Points_i}), \ $\xi_f=\bar\xi_k^0$ and  $\hat s_i(\xi_f)=0$ lead to $0=\hat s_i(\bar\xi_k^0)=\delta s+s(\bar\xi_k^0)=\delta s+\1$, i.e.
\be
\delta s\equiv s_{i0}\!-\!s(\xi_0)=-\1.
\label{PhaseCond}
\ee
This is the {\bf phase condition} \cite{FioAAC22} that $\delta s$ must fulfill in order
to maximize $F$ (and the WFA) for fixed $\bar{n}$. 


