The results corresponding to each scenario are given in Tables \ref{tab:scenarioI}, \ref{tab:scenarioII}, \ref{tab:scenarioIII}, \ref{tab:scenarioIV}, \ref{tab:scenarioV}, \ref{tab:scenarioVI} in the appendix. We aggregate those results by computing a \emph{validity score} for each metric. This validity score is simply the number of scenarios in which a metric has the expected ground truth behavior. Those scores are given in Table \ref{tab:scores}. The TOP and TED indices are originally designed to evaluate functional diversity at the individual level. This is relevant in scenarios I, V, and VI, as we are considering only one individual per species. However, the change of abundances in scenarios II, III, and IV leads to poor results with these indices because they do not account for abundance values. We can see that no functional diversity index has passed all the tests. FaD, mFaD, FD, AC, and FR do not account for abundance values. This leads to very poor scores, especially for scenarios II, III, and IV. Rao's QE, FDis, and FDVar achieve the highest scores. They fail in scenarios I and VI because they do not fulfill the set monotonicity requirement for a functional diversity index. They remain the best choices to evaluate functional diversity. 

Choosing only one index to compute functional diversity seems to be difficult, as all three aspects of functional diversity---functional richness, functional divergence, and functional evenness---need to be captured.  

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Measuring functional diversity is critical for biodiversity. In this paper, we first reviewed the literature on functional diversity measurement. We then listed fourteen requirements that robust measures of functional diversity should meet and subsequently assessed fifteen widely used functional diversity metrics against these requirements, revealing that none fully meet all of them. We also tested these metrics across six ecosystem scenarios, and again found no single metric that consistently exhibited the expected ground-truth behavior in all scenarios. Our results highlight the urgent need for developing a new generation of functional diversity metrics that more accurately reflect ecological realities.

