Finally, let us calculate the threshold for this code. As usual, suppose the physical qubits independently experience a single qubit Pauli error with probability $p$. The probability that the code is successful is 
\begin{equation}
    p_{success}=(1-p)^5+5p(1-p)^4,
\end{equation}
and so the probability that the code fails is
\begin{equation}
    p_{fail}=1-p_{success}=1-(1-p)^5-5p(1-p)^4.
\end{equation}
Then the (pseudo) threshold of the code can be solved for numerically, producing $p\approx0.1311$. Thus, the 5-qubit code lowers the error rate when the error rate of the physical qubits is below $\approx13.11\%$ (again, this assumes depolarizing type noise, perfect measurements, etc.).
